profile2=[(0,20),(.2,29),(.4,48),(.6,30),(.8,5),(1,5)]
add(703,'A catalyst changes the barrier while the endpoint stays the same','C1.1','C1.1.1–3, C1.1.10',
'The figure models the same catabolic reaction with and without an enzyme. Reactants have relative energy 20 kJ mol−1, products 5 kJ mol−1, and the two highest energies are 90 and 48 kJ mol−1. The horizontal coordinate is reaction progress, not time.',
[P('Calculate both forward activation energies and the overall energy change.',M('Subtract reactant energy from each peak: 90−20 and 48−20.'),A('The barriers are 70 and 28 kJ mol−1.'),A('Overall products−reactants = 5−20 = −15 kJ mol−1.')),
 P('Explain which curve is enzyme-catalysed and why more product per unit time is expected at the same temperature.',A('The curve with peak 48 kJ mol−1 is enzyme-catalysed.'),R('The lower activation barrier allows a larger fraction of substrate collisions to lead to reaction at the same temperature.')),
 P('Evaluate the statements “the enzyme supplies the released energy” and “the graph shows a shorter reaction time”.',R('Both curves have the same reactant and product energies, so the enzyme does not supply the net released energy.'),R('An enzyme is regenerated and provides an alternative catalytic pathway rather than being a consumed energy source.'),R('The x-axis is reaction progress; a reaction duration cannot be read from it.'))],
graph('Alternative pathways for one reaction',[('Path 1',profile1),('Path 2',profile2)],(0,1),(0,100),'Reaction progress / arbitrary units','Relative energy / kJ mol−1'),
[ck('Uncatalysed forward barrier',90-20,70),ck('Catalysed forward barrier',48-20,28),ck('Overall change',5-20,-15)],'Catalysis alters activation energy but not reactant/product energy difference. Reaction progress is not elapsed time.','Barriers 70 and 28 kJ mol−1; overall −15 kJ mol−1; curve 2 is catalysed.')

add(704,'Correcting a seed respirometer for a drifting control','C1.2','C1.2.4–6',
'A sealed respirometer contains 2.00 g germinating seeds and a CO2 absorber separated from the seeds. A matched control contains glass beads of equal displaced volume and the same absorber. Over 12.0 min the capillary marker moves 18.0 mm towards the seed chamber and 3.0 mm towards the control chamber. The capillary internal cross-sectional area is 0.800 mm². Temperature is kept constant.',
[P('Calculate corrected oxygen uptake in mm³ min−1 g−1, assuming marker movement measures gas-volume loss.',M('Corrected displacement = 18.0−3.0 = 15.0 mm; volume = 15.0×0.800 = 12.0 mm³.'),A('Rate = 12.0/(12.0×2.00) = 0.500 mm³ min−1 g−1.')),
 P('Explain the functions of the absorber and control.',R('The absorber removes respiratory CO2 so oxygen consumption produces a measurable gas-volume decrease.'),R('The bead control estimates shared non-biological pressure/volume drift without respiring tissue.')),
 P('Assess two improvements and one limitation when comparing seeds at 15°C and 25°C.',R('Allow each sealed apparatus to equilibrate at its test temperature before resetting the marker; temperature itself changes gas volume.'),R('Use equal seed dry mass or a clearly defined mass basis, similar germination stage and independent replicate chambers.'),R('Oxygen uptake estimates aerobic respiration under these conditions; it does not directly measure all ATP production or anaerobic respiration.'))],
svg(box(90,155,230,180,['sealed seeds','CO2 absorber below'])+box(535,155,230,180,['glass beads','CO2 absorber below'])+ln(320,230,435,230)+ln(765,230,865,230)+circle(395,230,8,'#111')+circle(838,230,8,'#111')+arr(420,185,360,185)+arr(860,185,815,185)+text(385,155,'18.0 mm',18)+text(818,155,'3.0 mm',18)+text(450,425,'Matched chambers in a constant-temperature water bath',20),'Paired respirometer model'),
[ck('Corrected displacement',18-3,15),ck('Mass normalized rate',(18-3)*.8/12/2,.5)],'CO2 absorption and gas-volume drift control are needed before interpreting uptake; no fixed ATP/O2 ratio is assumed.','Corrected uptake 0.500 mm³ min−1 g−1; control gas drift and standardize tissue.')

add(705,'The same ATP demand can require different glucose consumption','C1.2','C1.2.1–5',
'A constructed yeast comparison uses equal cell numbers. In the oxygenated condition, 0.400 mmol glucose is consumed per hour. In the oxygen-free condition, 3.20 mmol is consumed per hour. For this model only, measured usable ATP yields are 28 ATP per glucose with oxygen and 2 ATP per glucose without oxygen; these are supplied experimental values, not universal yields.',
[P('Calculate ATP production in mmol ATP h−1 for both conditions and the ratio of glucose consumption.',M('Multiply each glucose rate by its supplied ATP yield.'),A('Oxygenated: 0.400×28 = 11.2 mmol ATP h−1; oxygen-free: 3.20×2 = 6.40 mmol ATP h−1.'),A('Oxygen-free glucose consumption is 3.20/0.400 = 8.00 times as large.')),
 P('Explain why a larger glucose consumption rate does not imply a larger ATP production rate.',R('Anaerobic respiration gives less ATP per glucose because breakdown is incomplete and energy remains in products.'),R('The supplied yield difference exceeds the eightfold glucose-rate difference, so total ATP production is lower without oxygen.')),
 P('Calculate the glucose-consumption rate needed to meet an ATP demand of 11.2 mmol h−1 without oxygen. Predict one consequence if that rate cannot be sustained, and distinguish cellular respiration from gas exchange.',M('Required glucose = 11.2/2 = 5.60 mmol h−1.'),R('If glucose supply or the anaerobic pathway cannot support that rate, ATP-consuming processes such as active transport or biosynthesis must slow.'),R('Cell respiration transfers chemical energy to ATP; ATP hydrolysis then supplies energy to cellular work rather than respiration being synonymous with gas exchange.'))],
bars('Glucose consumption by equal yeast cell populations',['Oxygenated','Oxygen-free'],[.4,3.2],'Glucose / mmol h−1',4),
[ck('Oxygenated ATP',.4*28,11.2),ck('Anaerobic ATP',3.2*2,6.4),ck('Glucose ratio',3.2/.4,8),ck('Required glucose',11.2/2,5.6)],'Shared content compares ATP transfer and aerobic/anaerobic yield; glycolysis, NAD and fermentation mechanisms are not required. Supplied yields are explicitly conditional.','11.2 versus 6.40 mmol ATP h−1; anaerobic demand matching would need 5.60 mmol glucose h−1.')

add(706,'A light-response curve contains respiration as well as photosynthesis','C1.3','C1.3.1–3, C1.3.7–8',
'A leaf chamber measures net CO2 uptake per leaf area. At light intensities 0, 100, 200, 400 and 800 µmol photons m−2 s−1, net uptake is −2, 1, 4, 7 and 7 µmol CO2 m−2 s−1. Assume leaf respiration releases 2 µmol CO2 m−2 s−1 throughout this short investigation. At intensity 800, raising external CO2 increases net uptake to 11.',
[P('Calculate gross photosynthetic uptake at light intensities 0 and 400, and estimate the light compensation intensity by linear interpolation from 0 to 100.',M('Gross photosynthesis = net uptake + respiratory release.'),A('Gross rates are 0 and 9 µmol CO2 m−2 s−1.'),A('Compensation occurs at (2/3)×100 = 66.7 µmol photons m−2 s−1.')),
 P('Explain what the plateau and additional CO2 treatment show about limiting factors.',R('Light is limiting on the rising part because increased intensity increases net uptake.'),R('At high light, the rise from 7 to 11 after CO2 addition shows that CO2 availability limited uptake under the original conditions.')),
 P('Evaluate two assumptions needed before comparing gross photosynthesis across treatments.',R('Respiration may change with leaf temperature or metabolic state, so the stated constant-respiration assumption should be checked.'),R('Keep leaf area, temperature and water supply comparable; increased lamp heating can otherwise confound a light treatment.'),R('Use independent leaves and replicated CO2 treatments; one response curve does not establish population-level significance.'))],
graph('Net CO2 exchange at two high-light CO2 treatments',[('Original CO2',[(0,-2),(100,1),(200,4),(400,7),(800,7)]),('Raised CO2',[(800,11)])],(0,800),(-4,12),'Light intensity / µmol photons m−2 s−1','Net CO2 uptake / µmol m−2 s−1'),
[ck('Gross at400',7+2,9),ck('Compensation intensity',2/3*100,66.6666666667)],'Net exchange subtracts respiration; compensation is zero net rather than zero gross photosynthesis. No Calvin-cycle mechanisms are required at SL.','Gross 0 and 9 µmol m−2 s−1; compensation 66.7; CO2 limits the high-light plateau.')

