add(707,'One pigment band in one solvent is not a secure identification','C1.3','C1.3.4–6',
'The schematic chromatography strip shows distances from the origin. The solvent front moved 80 mm and bands A, B and C moved 20, 43 and 64 mm. Reference pigment P has Rf 0.54 in this solvent and Q has Rf 0.80. In a second solvent the sample band originally called B separates into two bands.',
[P('Calculate the three Rf values and identify the provisional matches.',M('Rf = distance moved by pigment / distance moved by solvent front.'),A('A = 0.250, B = 0.538, C = 0.800.'),A('B is provisionally consistent with P and C with Q in the first solvent.')),
 P('Explain the implication of B separating in the second solvent.',R('The original B band can contain at least two components that travelled together in the first solvent.'),R('An Rf match in one solvent is evidence for a possible identity, not proof of chemical purity or a unique pigment.')),
 P('Design a stronger test linking a separated pigment to photosynthesis.',R('Run authentic standards and sample together under identical solvent, paper and temperature conditions.'),R('Measure the isolated pigment’s absorption spectrum and compare wavelengths with a properly normalized photosynthetic action spectrum.'),R('A correlation between absorption and action supports a role but does not prove the pigment alone drives every photosynthetic step; other pigments can transfer absorbed energy.'))],
svg(chrom,'Pigment separation with distances measured from the origin'),
[ck('RfA',20/80,.25),ck('RfB',43/80,.5375),ck('RfC',64/80,.8)],'Rf is dimensionless and uses common origin distances. Co-migration is not identity or purity proof; absorption and action spectra are distinct.','Rf 0.250/0.538/0.800; B and C are provisional matches only.')

add(708,'Comparing equal-photon action and absorption spectra','C1.3','C1.3.5–8',
'At 430, 480, 550, 620 and 680 nm, a pigment extract has relative absorbance 0.90, 0.55, 0.10, 0.40 and 0.80. Intact leaves supplied with equal photon flux at each wavelength produce oxygen at rates 18, 14, 4, 10 and 17 arbitrary units. Leaf area, temperature and CO2 are held constant.',
[P('Compare the two spectra using two paired observations.',A('Both have relatively high values at 430 and 680 nm and a low value at 550 nm.'),A('At 480 nm, absorbance is lower than at 680 nm (0.55 versus 0.80), and oxygen production is also lower (14 versus 17).')),
 P('Calculate the action-rate ratio at 430 versus 550 nm and explain why green light still gives a non-zero result.',M('Use 18/4.'),A('The oxygen-production ratio is 4.50.'),R('The extract still absorbs some green light and intact leaves contain several pigments; low absorption does not mean no energy is absorbed.')),
 P('Evaluate the claim that red photons are always the only useful greenhouse illumination.',R('Blue light also has a high measured action rate, so the data do not support an exclusively red requirement.'),R('Equal photon flux makes this comparison meaningful, but these are selected wavelengths under one set of conditions; whole-growth responses need further testing.'),R('Use replicated plants under equal total photon supply and controlled temperature/CO2, then measure growth or carbon gain rather than inferring it solely from one short-term rate.'))],
graph('Action spectrum of intact leaves',[('Oxygen rate',[(430,18),(480,14),(550,4),(620,10),(680,17)])],(400,700),(0,20),'Wavelength / nm','Oxygen production / relative units',xticks=[430,480,550,620,680]),
[ck('Blue green action ratio',18/4,4.5),yes('Both sampled peaks exceed green',18>4 and 17>4)],'Equal photon flux is specified. Extract absorption is not identical to intact-leaf action, and short-term oxygen rate is not whole-plant growth.','Shared high blue/red response, low green response; blue:green action ratio 4.50.')

add(709,'Conduction speed and myelination need a controlled comparison','C2.2','C2.2.1–4',
'Four axon preparations have the following diameter, myelination and conduction speed: A, 2 µm, unmyelinated, 2 m s−1; B, 8 µm, unmyelinated, 8 m s−1; C, 2 µm, myelinated, 14 m s−1; D, 8 µm, myelinated, 56 m s−1. A stimulus is recorded 0.140 m along C after a short fixed equipment delay that has already been subtracted.',
[P('Calculate the conduction time along C and compare C with A.',M('Time = distance/speed = 0.140/14.'),A('Time = 0.0100 s = 10.0 ms.'),A('At equal diameter, C conducts seven times as fast as A.')),
 P('Use two comparisons to separate the effects of diameter and myelination.',A('Among unmyelinated axons, increasing diameter from 2 to 8 µm increases speed from 2 to 8 m s−1.'),A('At diameter 8 µm, myelination is associated with an increase from 8 to 56 m s−1.')),
 P('Explain why a mixed-axon correlation cannot by itself measure the causal effect of diameter, and identify the basic path of a neural message.',R('Diameter and myelination both vary; a correlation using all four points confounds their separate contributions.'),R('Use comparable axons with the same myelination status and control temperature when testing diameter effects.'),R('Inputs are received through dendrites/cell body and an action potential propagates along the axon toward terminal synapses; detailed channel sequencing is not required here.'))],
graph('Speed at two diameters',[('Unmyelinated',[(2,2),(8,8)]),('Myelinated',[(2,14),(8,56)])],(0,10),(0,60),'Axon diameter / µm','Conduction speed / m s−1'),
[ck('Conduction seconds',.14/14,.01),ck('Myelin speedratio',14/2,7)],'Shared syllabus assesses comparative speed and neuron organization; no saltatory/local-current mechanism or oscilloscope-event recall is demanded.','10.0 ms; equal-diameter myelination comparison is sevenfold, with diameter controlled.')

add(710,'Calcium removal separates arrival from transmitter release','C2.2','C2.2.5–7',
'In a chemical synapse model, an action potential is detected at the presynaptic terminal in every treatment. Vesicle-fusion counts per terminal over the same interval are: normal external Ca2+, 24; Ca2+-free medium, 2; Ca2+-free medium plus acetylcholine applied directly to the postsynaptic membrane, 2. Postsynaptic depolarization occurs in the first and third treatments but not the second. The third treatment does not restore vesicle fusion.',
[P('Calculate the percentage decrease in vesicle fusion on removing Ca2+.',M('Decrease = (24−2)/24×100.'),A('Vesicle fusion decreases by 91.7%.')),
 P('Deduce the stage impaired by calcium removal and explain the direct-acetylcholine result.',R('Action-potential arrival is retained, but fusion falls; the evidence places the main impairment between arrival and transmitter release.'),R('Ca2+ entry into the terminal normally triggers vesicle fusion and transmitter release.'),R('Applied acetylcholine bypasses impaired presynaptic release and can still act on functional postsynaptic receptors.')),
 P('Explain normal transmission direction and evaluate what this experiment does not show.',R('Vesicles release transmitter from the presynaptic side; receptors on the postsynaptic membrane respond to diffusion across the cleft.'),R('Binding of acetylcholine opens channels allowing positive ions to enter and depolarize the postsynaptic membrane.'),R('Counts from one model do not establish universal effect size or prove every other presynaptic process is unaffected; independent terminals and controls are needed.'))],
svg(box(100,100,270,150,['Presynaptic terminal','action potential arrives'])+box(100,340,670,110,['Postsynaptic membrane','acetylcholine receptors'])+circle(180,225,12)+circle(245,225,12)+arr(215,252,215,325)+arr(600,160,600,325)+text(610,140,'direct acetylcholine',18)+text(450,290,'synaptic cleft',18),'Interventions on opposite sides of a chemical synapse'),
[ck('Fusionpercentagefall',100*(24-2)/24,91.6666666667)],'Ca-triggered release and excitatory acetylcholine synapses are shared. Bypass rescue locates a defect without claiming perfect molecular specificity.','Fusion falls 91.7%; direct transmitter bypasses the Ca-dependent release stage.')

add(711,'Blood chemistry coordinates ventilation and circulation','C3.1','C3.1.12, C3.1.14–15',
'In a short model investigation, increased inspired CO2 raises arterial CO2 while oxygen is held near its starting value. At minutes 0, 2, 4 and 6, ventilation rates are 6, 9, 12 and 10 dm³ min−1 and heart rates are 70, 79, 88 and 82 min−1. At minute 4, arterial pH is lower than at minute 0. The gas treatment ends after minute 4.',
[P('Calculate the percentage increase in ventilation and heart rate from minute 0 to minute 4.',M('Use (final−initial)/initial×100 for each variable.'),A('Ventilation rises 100%; heart rate rises 25.7%.')),
 P('Explain a feedback route linking elevated CO2 to increased ventilation.',R('More CO2 in blood increases acidity and lowers pH; chemoreceptors, including those in the brainstem, detect the change.'),R('Respiratory centres in the brainstem send nerve signals to diaphragm and intercostal muscles, increasing ventilation.'),R('Greater CO2 removal opposes the initial rise, so this component is negative feedback.')),
 P('Distinguish this control from baroreceptor feedback and explain why both systems can affect the heart.',R('Baroreceptors in the carotid/aortic regions monitor blood pressure rather than directly monitoring CO2.'),R('Chemoreceptor and baroreceptor input is integrated by the medulla, which adjusts cardiac activity through nerve signals.'),R('Transport and ventilation must be coordinated to deliver oxygen and remove respiratory CO2; a matching time trend alone does not prove one measured rate directly causes the other.'))],
graph('Coordinated responses during a CO2 intervention',[('Heart rate',[(0,70),(2,79),(4,88),(6,82)])],(0,6),(60,100),'Time / min','Heart rate / min−1'),
[ck('Ventilation percent increase',100*(12-6)/6,100),ck('Heart rate percent increase',100*(88-70)/70,25.7142857143)],'CO2/pH feedback is separated from pressure sensing; the medulla integrates signals. Time correlation is not proof of a direct rate-to-rate causal link.','Ventilation +100%; heart rate +25.7%; CO2 removal closes negative feedback.')

reflex_nodes=[(45,135,175,90,['Pain receptor','in hand']),(330,135,175,90,['Sensory route','S']),(640,135,190,90,['Interneuron','spinal cord']),(640,340,190,90,['Motor route','M']),(330,340,175,90,['Flexor muscle','effector'])]
add(712,'A withdrawal reflex can survive loss of conscious sensation','C3.1','C3.1.4–10',
'The schematic shows a pain-withdrawal pathway with one spinal interneuron. Route S enters the spinal cord; route M leaves it. A separate ascending pathway carries information from the cord to the cerebral hemispheres. In a hypothetical lesion, the ascending pathway is interrupted above the entry level while S, the interneuron, M and the muscle remain functional.',
[P('Predict whether rapid withdrawal and conscious perception of this stimulus are retained, explaining the distinction.',A('The local withdrawal reflex can remain, while conscious perception carried by the interrupted route is lost.'),R('The spinal cord integrates the local sensory–interneuron–motor pathway without requiring a conscious decision first.'),R('Conscious perception requires information to reach the appropriate cerebral regions.')),
 P('Predict the effects of cutting S or cutting M in separate trials.',A('Cutting S prevents this stimulus from reaching the spinal reflex circuit, so the specified withdrawal is absent.'),A('Cutting M prevents the output reaching the flexor, so withdrawal is absent even if sensory information still reaches the cord.')),
 P('Explain why a nerve cross-section can contain both sensory and motor fibres and why the cerebellum has a different role.',R('A peripheral nerve is a bundle of axons; different fibres can carry sensory input toward the CNS and motor output away.'),R('An impulse in an individual fibre has a defined propagating path; a mixed nerve is not one axon carrying both functions.'),R('The cerebellum coordinates movement and balance, whereas the local spinal circuit can initiate this simple involuntary withdrawal.'))],
pathway('Local spinal pathway with a separate ascending branch',reflex_nodes,[(0,1,''),(1,2,''),(2,3,''),(3,4,'')],['Ascending pathway to brain is separate and interrupted in the stated lesion.']),
[yes('Local circuit remains intact under stated lesion',len(reflex_nodes)==5)],'A single interneuron pain reflex and mixed peripheral nerves are current shared content. A lesion is hypothetical and does not imply normal complete loss of every form of sensation.','Local withdrawal retained; specified conscious route lost; cutting either sensory input or motor output blocks withdrawal.')

add(713,'Relative vaccine effectiveness differs from absolute risk reduction','C3.2','C3.2.14–18',
'In a constructed randomized trial, 10 000 participants receive a vaccine and 10 000 receive placebo. During the same follow-up, symptomatic infection occurs in 120 vaccinated and 300 placebo participants. Define relative effectiveness = 1−(vaccinated risk/placebo risk). A supplied 95% confidence interval for the risk ratio is 0.32–0.50; a ratio of 1 is the no-difference value. Allocation is concealed and outcome assessors are blinded.',
[P('Calculate both infection risks, relative effectiveness and absolute risk reduction in percentage points.',M('Risks are 120/10 000 = 0.012 and 300/10 000 = 0.030.'),A('Relative effectiveness = 1−0.012/0.030 = 0.600 = 60.0%.'),A('Absolute reduction = 3.00%−1.20% = 1.80 percentage points.')),
 P('Interpret the supplied confidence interval without using error-bar overlap as a test.',R('The interval lies below the no-difference ratio of 1, supporting lower symptomatic risk for the vaccinated group under the stated analysis.'),R('It does not mean that 95% of individuals have risk ratios in that interval or that protection is complete.')),
 P('Explain two design strengths and one limit to generalization.',R('Random allocation reduces systematic baseline confounding, while concealed allocation limits selection into groups.'),R('Blinded outcome assessment reduces differential classification of symptoms/infection.'),R('The result applies to the studied population, outcome and follow-up; it alone does not establish the same effectiveness against every infection, future variant or time since vaccination.'))],
bars('Symptomatic cases under equal follow-up',['Vaccinated','Placebo'],[120,300],'Cases per 10 000',400),
[ck('Risk ratio',120/300,.4),ck('Effectiveness percent',(1-120/300)*100,60),ck('Absolute reduction percentage points',(300-120)/10000*100,1.8)],'The trial is fictional and educational. Relative reduction, absolute percentage-point reduction and the supplied inferential interval are distinct. No clinical recommendation is made.','Risks 1.20%/3.00%; relative effectiveness 60.0%; absolute reduction 1.80 percentage points.')

add(714,'A second exposure tests antigen-specific memory','C3.2','C3.2.6–11, C3.2.14',
'A model organism receives antigen A at day 0. At day 28 it receives A again and a novel antigen B for the first time. Antibody concentrations to A peak at 20 units after the first dose and 120 units after the second. Antibody to B peaks at 24 units after its first dose. Antigen-specific assays do not cross-react.',
[P('Calculate the ratio of the second A peak to its first peak and compare the response to A with B after day 28.',M('Use 120/20.'),A('The second A peak is 6.00 times the first.'),A('After day 28, A rises sooner and reaches a much higher peak than B, although both antigens are delivered at the same time.')),
 P('Explain the cellular basis of this contrast.',R('The first A exposure selects A-specific lymphocytes, which proliferate and form plasma cells and memory cells.'),R('A-specific memory cells remain and respond rapidly on re-exposure, producing many antibody-secreting cells.'),R('B is novel, so its specific lymphocyte population must undergo a primary response; A memory is not automatically memory to unrelated B.')),
 P('Evaluate what antibody concentration alone cannot establish about protection.',R('Binding antibodies do not necessarily all neutralize the pathogen; concentration alone is not a complete functional protection assay.'),R('The model shows antigen-specific humoral memory but does not measure all cellular defences, exposure differences or duration of protection.'))],
graph('Antigen-specific antibody responses',[('Antibody A',[(0,0),(7,4),(14,20),(21,10),(28,4),(32,75),(36,120),(42,95),(50,65)]),('Antibody B',[(0,0),(28,0),(32,2),(36,9),(42,24),(50,12)])],(0,50),(0,140),'Time / days','Antibody concentration / relative units',xticks=[0,14,28,42,50]),
[ck('A peak fold change',120/20,6),ck('A second toBfirst',120/24,5)],'Memory specificity, clonal selection and plasma-cell antibodies are shared; antibody amount is not equated with complete clinical protection.','A response is sixfold its primary peak and faster than the novel B response; memory is specific.')

plate=''
positions=[(0,-75),(-65,-35),(60,-25),(-45,50),(55,65)]
for cx,label in [(235,'Replicate 1'),(660,'Replicate 2')]:
    plate+=circle(cx,260,135)+text(cx,95,label,21)
    plate+=ln(cx-8,110,cx+8,110)
    for dx,dy in positions:plate+=circle(cx+dx,260+dy,9,'#111')
plate+=text(450,465,'Same orientation; resistant colonies appear in the same source positions.',19)
add(715,'Replica patterns distinguish selection from a directed mutation claim','C3.2','C3.2.12–13',
'Colonies grow first on an antibiotic-free master plate. A sterile transfer copies their positions to two plates containing antibiotic. Resistant colonies repeatedly appear at matching positions, as shown. In a separate culture, resistant bacteria increase from 0.10% to 80.0% after many generations with antibiotic; total cell numbers and growth need not remain constant.',
[P('Explain why the replicated pattern supports resistance existing before antibiotic exposure.',R('Matching positions trace resistant descendants to the same original colonies on the antibiotic-free master plate.'),R('Independent directed mutations caused by contact with the drug would not specifically predict repeated matching source positions.')),
 P('Calculate the fold increase in resistant proportion and its increase in percentage points.',M('Compare proportions using 80.0/0.10, while percentage points use subtraction.'),A('The resistant proportion increases 800-fold; the increase is 79.9 percentage points.')),
 P('Explain natural selection and antibiotic selectivity, and identify a limit of these data.',R('Heritable resistance variants survive or reproduce better when susceptible bacteria are inhibited, so their descendants become more common.'),R('The antibiotic selects existing variation rather than instructing mutations to meet bacterial needs.'),R('Antibiotics target bacterial structures/processes absent or sufficiently different in host cells; they do not target viral replication in the same way.'),R('Proportions alone do not reveal the absolute numbers of resistant cells; total counts and independent cultures are required.'))],
svg(plate,'Replicate antibiotic plates copied from one drug-free master'),
[ck('Resistance proportionfold',80/.10,800),ck('Percentagepoint increase',80-.10,79.9)],'Matching replica positions support pre-existing variants; selection is not directed mutagenesis. Proportion is explicitly separated from absolute abundance.','800-fold proportional rise and 79.9 percentage points; selection favours pre-existing resistant lineages.')

cmrbody=text(245,95,'First sample: mark all animals',20)+text(665,95,'Second sample after mixing',20)
cmrbody+='<rect x="90" y="125" width="300" height="280" rx="10" fill="white" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="515" y="125" width="300" height="280" rx="10" fill="white" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'
for i in range(20):
    x=125+55*(i%5);y=175+55*(i//5)
    cmrbody+=circle(x,y,10,'#111')+ln(x-5,y,x+5,y,w=2)
for i in range(25):
    x=550+55*(i%5);y=165+45*(i//5)
    cmrbody+=circle(x,y,10,'#111' if i<5 else 'white')
cmrbody+=arr(405,260,500,260)+text(450,235,'mix',18)+text(450,465,'Each symbol represents 4 animals; filled symbols are marked.',19)+text(450,505,'First sample 80; second sample 100, including 20 marked.',18)
cmr=svg(cmrbody,'Marked and unmarked fractions in two samples')
add(716,'Immigration can bias a capture–mark–recapture estimate','C4.1','C4.1.4–6',
'In a closed-population model, 80 animals are captured, marked and released. After mixing, 100 animals are captured and 20 are marked. Use N≈MC/R, where M is originally marked, C is the second sample and R is its marked count. A second scenario adds unmarked immigrants before recapture, while marked residents remain; the estimate then concerns an intended original resident population.',
[P('Estimate population size and the unmarked proportion in the second sample.',M('N≈80×100/20.'),A('Estimated population = 400 animals; the sample is 80.0% unmarked.')),
 P('Explain the expected direction of bias for the original population if immigration dilutes the marked fraction.',R('Adding unmarked animals reduces the marked proportion R/C, if the capture process is otherwise comparable.'),R('Because N≈M/(R/C), the estimate increases and overestimates the original resident population.')),
 P('Evaluate three further assumptions and propose how to check one.',R('Marks should remain detectable and not change survival, behaviour or capture probability.'),R('Marked animals should mix with unmarked animals, and individuals should have similar capture probabilities for the estimator used.'),R('Births, deaths and boundary movements should be negligible between occasions for a closed-population interpretation.'),R('A short pilot can compare marked versus unmarked survival/capture or assess mixing by recapture locations; repeat sampling with a justified design rather than assuming closure.'))],
cmr,[ck('Lincoln estimate',80*100/20,400),ck('Unmarked percent',(100-20)/100*100,80)],'The Lincoln estimator is supplied. Immigration is interpreted relative to the explicitly intended original population, avoiding ambiguity about current versus original abundance.','400 animals; unmarked immigration lowers marked fraction and biases the original-population estimate high.')

add(717,'Unequal habitat areas require a weighted quadrat estimate','C4.1','C4.1.2–3',
'A 1200 m² reserve contains 900 m² dry habitat and 300 m² damp habitat. Five randomly located 1.00 m² quadrats in the dry area contain 1, 2, 2, 3 and 2 plants; five in the damp area contain 8, 10, 12, 9 and 11. Quadrats do not overlap. The schematic shows the habitat boundary; points are sampling positions, not individual plants.',
[P('Estimate total plants using the two habitat means and compare it with pooling all ten quadrats without weighting.',M('Dry mean = 2.00 m−2; damp mean = 10.0 m−2; multiply each by its habitat area.'),A('Stratified estimate = 2×900 + 10×300 = 4800 plants.'),A('Unweighted pooled mean = 6.00 m−2 gives 7200 plants, an overestimate of 2400.')),
 P('Explain the source of the discrepancy.',R('Half the sampled quadrats come from damp habitat although damp habitat is only one quarter of the reserve.'),R('Pooling without area weighting over-represents the high-density damp habitat.')),
 P('Propose a stronger estimate with uncertainty and define the sampling unit.',R('Use more independently randomized quadrat positions in each habitat, retaining area weights; effort can be allocated by area and observed variation.'),R('The quadrat is the sampling unit; treating each counted plant as an independent spatial sample would inflate replication.'),R('Report habitat variation and uncertainty of the weighted estimate; random positioning reduces selection bias but does not ensure one small sample is exact.'))],
svg('<rect x="100" y="120" width="690" height="300" fill="white" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'+ln(618,120,618,420)+text(350,95,'Dry: 900 m²',21)+text(705,95,'Damp: 300 m²',21)+''.join(circle(x,y,7,'#111') for x,y in [(170,170),(310,300),(500,210),(230,370),(540,355),(655,185),(735,265),(670,370),(755,340),(710,145)]),'Stratified random quadrat positions in unequal habitats'),
[ck('Dry density',statistics.mean([1,2,2,3,2]),2),ck('Damp density',statistics.mean([8,10,12,9,11]),10),ck('Weighted plants',2*900+10*300,4800),ck('Pooledplants',6*1200,7200)],'Habitat area weighting is essential. Dots are sampling positions and quadrats, not plants, are independent units. No SD formula recall is required.','Weighted 4800 plants; unweighted pooling gives 7200 because damp habitat is oversampled.')

add(718,'Species association is evidence to investigate, not proof of mutualism','C4.1','C4.1.10–13',
'In 100 independently sampled quadrats, species X and Y are both present in 30, X alone in 10, Y alone in 20 and neither in 40. Test independence with χ²=Σ(O−E)²/E; expected count E=(row total×column total)/100. Use 1 degree of freedom and critical χ²=3.84 at p=0.05. The figure plots the four observed counts. Both species might also respond to soil moisture.',
[P('Calculate the four expected counts and χ², then state the statistical conclusion under the supplied test.',M('X present total=40; Y present total=50, so expected counts are 20, 20, 30 and 30 in the stated order.'),A('χ² = 100/20 + 100/20 + 100/30 + 100/30 = 16.7.'),A('16.7 exceeds 3.84, so reject independence at the stated threshold.')),
 P('Describe the association and explain why it does not prove mutualism.',R('Co-occurrence is more frequent than expected under independence: 30 observed versus 20 expected.'),R('Shared preference for moisture could generate the pattern without either species benefiting the other.')),
 P('Design a test that distinguishes a direct beneficial interaction from the shared-moisture explanation.',R('Randomize addition/removal or presence/absence of X in matched plots while independently controlling or crossing soil moisture treatments.'),R('Measure Y survival/growth/reproduction, use replicate independent plots and include handling controls.'),R('To claim mutualism, also test a benefit of Y to X; association or a one-way benefit is insufficient.'))],
bars('Observed quadrat categories',['Both','X only','Y only','Neither'],[30,10,20,40],'Number of quadrats',50),
[ck('Expected both',40*50/100,20),ck('Chi square',100/20+100/20+100/30+100/30,16.6666666667),yes('Reject supplied independence threshold',16.6666666667>3.84)],'Expected frequencies all exceed five. Supplied test assumptions and threshold are used; association does not establish causation or reciprocal benefit.','χ²=16.7, reject independence; positive association can arise from shared habitat preference.')

foodnodes=[(45,140,180,100,['Grass NPP','12 000']),(360,140,190,100,['Herbivore','ingestion 2400']),(680,140,175,100,['Predator','ingestion 180']),(360,350,240,100,['Detrital pathway','unconsumed + egested'])]
add(719,'Energy must be converted to production before transfer efficiency','C4.2','C4.2.3–5, C4.2.11–16',
'All fluxes in this constructed grassland budget are kJ m−2 year−1. Grass net primary production is 12 000. Herbivores ingest 2400, egest 900 and use 1100 in respiration; the remaining assimilated energy becomes production. Predators ingest 180 of herbivore production. No other herbivore losses are included.',
[P('Calculate herbivore assimilation, production and production transfer efficiency from grass NPP.',M('Assimilation = 2400−900 = 1500; production = 1500−1100.'),A('Herbivore production = 400 kJ m−2 year−1.'),A('Production transfer efficiency = 400/12 000×100 = 3.33%.')),
 P('Calculate the fraction of herbivore production ingested by predators and explain why it is not predator production efficiency.',M('Use 180/400×100.'),A('Predators ingest 45.0% of herbivore production.'),R('Predator egestion and respiration have not been subtracted, so predator production cannot be calculated from ingestion alone.')),
 P('Explain the fate of two energy pathways omitted from the simple grazing chain.',R('Unconsumed plant biomass and herbivore egesta can enter detrital food webs and provide substrates for decomposers.'),R('Respiratory energy is dissipated as heat and is not recycled back into chemical energy by decomposers; elements can be recycled.'))],
pathway('Selected energy fluxes; units kJ m−2 year−1',foodnodes,[(0,1,'2400'),(1,2,'180'),(1,3,'900 egested')]),
[ck('Assimilation',2400-900,1500),ck('Herbivoreproduction',2400-900-1100,400),ck('Productionefficiencypercent',400/12000*100,3.33333333333),ck('Predatoringestionfractionpercent',180/400*100,45)],'Food-web arrows point from resource to consumer. Trophic production and ingestion efficiencies are explicitly distinguished, with consistent area/time units.','Assimilation 1500, production 400; transfer efficiency 3.33%; predators ingest 45.0% of herbivore production.')

carbonnodes=[(40,130,220,100,['Atmospheric CO2','external reservoir']),(355,130,220,100,['Producer carbon','gross uptake 900']),(650,130,210,100,['Consumers and','decomposers']),(355,340,220,100,['Harvest exported','40'])]
add(720,'A carbon budget separates plant production from ecosystem storage','C4.2','C4.2.8–10, C4.2.17–22',
'A field has annual gross carbon fixation 900 g C m−2 year−1. Producer respiration releases 360 and respiration by consumers/decomposers releases 450 in the same units. Harvest exports 40. Other imports/exports and changes in dissolved pools are negligible. Define net ecosystem production before harvest = gross fixation minus all ecosystem respiration.',
[P('Calculate net primary production, net ecosystem production before harvest and the annual carbon-stock change after harvest.',M('NPP = 900−360; ecosystem net = 900−360−450.'),A('NPP = 540 and net ecosystem production = 90 g C m−2 year−1.'),A('After harvest the stock increases by 90−40 = 50 g C m−2 year−1.')),
 P('Explain why positive NPP alone does not establish ecosystem carbon storage.',R('NPP subtracts producer respiration but not respiration by consumers and decomposers.'),R('A high heterotrophic respiration or export flux could exceed NPP and produce net carbon loss.')),
 P('Evaluate a claim that the field is a permanent carbon sink on this evidence.',R('This one-year budget supports a positive stock change under its assumptions; it does not establish permanence.'),R('Repeat across years and measure soil/biomass stocks and relevant lateral fluxes to check the budget and detect changing decomposition or harvest.'),R('Carbon atoms cycle between reservoirs, whereas energy associated with respiration is ultimately dissipated as heat; storing carbon is not perpetual energy recycling.'))],
pathway('Selected reservoirs and carbon transfers',carbonnodes,[(0,1,'fixation'),(1,2,'feeding/litter'),(1,3,'harvest')],['Respiration returns carbon from living compartments to the atmosphere.']),
[ck('NPP',900-360,540),ck('Netecosystemproduction',900-360-450,90),ck('Stockchange',900-360-450-40,50)],'Budget includes producer and heterotrophic respiration and harvest exports. Reservoir stock change is distinct from NPP and one year does not prove permanence.','NPP 540, ecosystem net 90, after-harvest stock gain 50 g C m−2 year−1.')

# Additional HL: actual additional understanding is required in every item.
rate0=lambda s:120*s/(4+s)
rateI=lambda s:120*s/(12+s)
add(721,'A statin-like inhibitor changes substrate competition','C1.1','C1.1.14–15',
'A purified enzyme has model rate v=120S/(4+S) without inhibitor and v=120S/(12+S) with inhibitor I. S is in mmol dm−3 and v in µmol min−1. Define Vmax as the limiting rate at very large S, and K½ as the substrate concentration giving half that limiting rate. The inhibitor is a reversible active-site-binding compound used as a statin-like model; the equation and definitions are supplied, not recall requirements.',
[P('Determine Vmax and K½ in both treatments, then calculate the rates at S=4.',M('Both expressions approach 120 as S becomes very large; half-rate requires S equal to the constant added to S in the denominator.'),A('Vmax = 120 µmol min−1 in both; K½ = 4 and 12 mmol dm−3.'),A('At S=4, rates are 60.0 without I and 30.0 µmol min−1 with I.')),
 P('Explain why high substrate concentration reduces this inhibitor’s effect.',R('Substrate and inhibitor bind reversibly to the active site, so they compete for the same enzyme population.'),R('Increasing substrate increases the chance that an active site is occupied by substrate rather than inhibitor; the same limiting rate can be approached.')),
 P('Contrast the mechanism and high-substrate prediction with reversible allosteric non-competitive inhibition.',R('An allosteric inhibitor binds a distinct specific site and changes enzyme conformation, impairing catalysis.'),R('Substrate cannot simply displace it from that distinct site, so high substrate does not fully restore the uninhibited maximum in the non-competitive model.'),R('The supplied fitted equations establish the model’s rate predictions; real samples need replicate initial-rate data and appropriate binding evidence before a mechanistic classification is claimed.'))],
graph('Supplied initial-rate models',[('No inhibitor',[(s,rate0(s)) for s in range(81)]),('Inhibitor I',[(s,rateI(s)) for s in range(81)])],(0,80),(0,130),'Substrate S / mmol dm−3','Initial rate / µmol min−1'),
[ck('Control rate at4',rate0(4),60),ck('Inhibited rate at4',rateI(4),30),ck('Control halfmaximum',rate0(4),120/2),ck('Inhibited halfmaximum',rateI(12),120/2)],'Competitive and allosteric inhibition are AHL. Vmax/K½ definitions and rate equations are supplied extensions; no memorized Km equation or reciprocal plot is demanded.','Same Vmax 120; K½ 4→12; rates at S=4 are 60.0→30.0 µmol min−1.')

add(722,'End-product feedback regulates a pathway before substrate is wasted','C1.1','C1.1.13–16',
'A simplified pathway converts threonine through intermediates to isoleucine. In separate assays of its first enzyme E1, activity is 10.0 units without added isoleucine, 3.0 with isoleucine, 3.2 with isoleucine plus tenfold threonine, and 9.6 after isoleucine is removed. E2 remains at 10.0 units. A binding experiment places isoleucine at a site distinct from E1’s active site.',
[P('Calculate inhibition of E1 in the isoleucine treatment and percentage activity recovered after removal.',M('Inhibition = (10.0−3.0)/10.0×100; recovered activity = 9.6/10.0×100.'),A('E1 is inhibited by 70.0%; activity after removal is 96.0% of control.')),
 P('Explain why these results support reversible feedback inhibition of E1.',R('Isoleucine is a pathway end product that specifically suppresses an earlier enzyme, reducing further synthesis when product accumulates.'),R('Binding at a distinct site and little rescue by extra threonine support allosteric rather than direct active-site competition.'),R('Recovery after removal supports reversible inhibition rather than permanent destruction of most enzyme.')),
 P('Explain the advantage of targeting an early enzyme and compare this pathway with a metabolic cycle.',R('Suppressing an early committed step avoids spending substrate and energy making unneeded downstream intermediates.'),R('The depicted pathway is linear because its end product does not regenerate its starting substrate as part of the sequence.'),R('In a cycle such as the Krebs or Calvin cycle, a starting acceptor is regenerated, allowing repeated operation while inputs enter and products leave.'))],
pathway('Simplified biosynthetic sequence',[(40,190,170,95,'Threonine'),(345,190,180,95,'Intermediate'),(665,190,190,95,'Isoleucine')],[(0,1,'E1'),(1,2,'E2')],['E1 binding experiment: end product binds a distinct site.']),
[ck('E1 inhibitionpercent',(10-3)/10*100,70),ck('Recoveredpercent',9.6/10*100,96)],'The current isoleucine feedback example is used. Distinct-site binding and reversibility are explicit evidence, rather than inferred solely from a low rate.','70.0% inhibition and 96.0% recovery; reversible allosteric end-product feedback targets E1.')

add(723,'Washout distinguishes a covalent inhibitor from reversible binding','C1.1','C1.1.15–17',
'Purified bacterial cell-wall cross-linking enzyme is incubated with a penicillin-like compound for 0, 5 or 10 min, then the free compound is removed before assay with abundant substrate. Activities are 90, 40 and 12 units. A matched no-compound sample remains at 90 units after the same handling. Analysis detects a covalent compound–enzyme adduct at the active site after washout. All conclusions concern this specified enzyme model.',
[P('Calculate percentage loss of activity after 10 min and explain why a no-compound handling control is needed.',M('Loss = (90−12)/90×100.'),A('Activity loss = 86.7%.'),R('The matched control tests whether incubation and compound-removal procedures themselves damage or remove enzyme.')),
 P('Explain how a mechanism-based inhibitor can produce the persistent loss.',R('The enzyme interacts with the inhibitor at its active site and catalysis leads to a chemical change/covalent adduct that inactivates the site.'),R('Removing free compound does not remove the covalently bound adduct, so existing modified enzyme remains inactive.'),R('This differs from a reversible competitive inhibitor, which can dissociate and be removed or outcompeted.')),
 P('Predict two outcomes and evaluate a limit of the assay.',R('Adding more substrate after washout will not simply restore activity of the covalently inactivated sites.'),R('Supplying newly synthesized unmodified enzyme can restore catalytic capacity if the free inhibitor has been removed.'),R('A purified-enzyme result does not alone establish a dose that is effective or safe in an organism; entry, metabolism and other targets are not measured.'))],
graph('Residual activity after removal of free compound',[('Compound preincubation',[(0,90),(5,40),(10,12)]),('Handling control',[(0,90),(10,90)])],(0,10),(0,100),'Preincubation / min','Activity after washout / units'),
[ck('Activitylosspercent',(90-12)/90*100,86.6666666667)],'Mechanism-based inhibition and penicillin are AHL. Covalent-adduct evidence is supplied; washout alone is not treated as proof of a particular chemical mechanism.','86.7% persistent loss; covalent active-site modification is not reversed by simply adding substrate.')

add(724,'Metabolic heat is a rate normalized to the amount of living material','C1.1','C1.1.11–12',
'Two yeast preparations are tested in insulated calorimeters of effective heat capacity 420 J °C−1. Preparation A contains 0.500 g dry biomass and warms by 0.80°C in 10.0 min; B contains 1.00 g and warms by 1.20°C. A matched killed-yeast blank warms by 0.10°C in each run. Use heat Q=CΔT and subtract the blank before normalizing. Heat loss is initially assumed negligible.',
[P('Calculate the corrected heat-production rates per gram for A and B.',M('Corrected temperature rises are 0.70 and 1.10°C; heats are 294 and 462 J.'),A('A: 294/(10.0×0.500)=58.8 J min−1 g−1.'),A('B: 462/(10.0×1.00)=46.2 J min−1 g−1.')),
 P('Explain why B warming more does not show greater activity per unit biomass and why heat accompanies metabolism.',R('B contains twice as much biomass; its greater total heat can coexist with a lower mass-specific rate.'),R('Metabolic energy transfer is not 100% efficient in capture as useful chemical work, and energy is dissipated as heat.')),
 P('Distinguish intracellular from extracellular catalysis and evaluate the heat-loss assumption.',R('Glycolysis and Krebs-cycle reactions occur inside cells, whereas secreted digestive enzymes can catalyse reactions outside cells.'),R('Heat leaving the calorimeter makes measured warming underestimate the true heat produced, even after subtracting a blank.'),R('Calibrate the apparatus, use comparable starting temperatures and mixing, and obtain independent biological replicates; an identical blank drift does not guarantee identical heat loss in different-temperature runs.'))],
bars('Measured temperature rises before blank correction',['A: 0.500 g','B: 1.00 g','Killed blank'],[.8,1.2,.1],'Temperature rise / °C',1.6),
[ck('A heatrate',420*(.8-.1)/10/.5,58.8),ck('B heatrate',420*(1.2-.1)/10,46.2)],'Metabolic heat and intra/extracellular catalysis are actual AHL content. Heat and ATP are not equated; blank drift is not a complete heat-loss correction.','A 58.8 and B 46.2 J min−1 g−1; B gives more total heat but less per biomass.')

add(725,'Glycolysis has an investment phase before its net ATP return','C1.2','C1.2.7–8',
'A glycolysis model starts with 1.00 mmol glucose in the cytoplasm. Two ATP are used per glucose before cleavage into two 3-carbon molecules. Subsequent reactions form two ATP and one reduced NAD per 3-carbon molecule and end with pyruvate. The diagram tracks carbon units, not named intermediate molecules.',
[P('Calculate pyruvate production, gross ATP formation, net ATP gain and reduced NAD formation.',M('Each glucose yields two 3-carbon branches; apply the supplied yield to both branches.'),A('Pyruvate = 2.00 mmol; gross ATP formed = 4.00 mmol; net ATP gain = 4.00−2.00 = 2.00 mmol.'),A('Reduced NAD formed = 2.00 mmol.')),
 P('Explain the linked oxidation and reduction in the pathway.',R('An organic intermediate is oxidized by transfer of hydrogen/electrons to NAD.'),R('NAD is reduced and can carry reducing power to later reactions; it is not the final carbon product.')),
 P('Predict the direct effect of a treatment that prevents ADP from accepting phosphate, assuming all other components are initially present.',R('The ATP-producing substrate-level phosphorylation reactions cannot continue normally without usable ADP.'),R('Gross ATP production and net gain fall; ATP used early must not be counted again as a net product.'),R('The model identifies cytoplasmic carbon breakdown but does not imply that glycolysis alone fully oxidizes glucose or requires oxygen directly.'))],
pathway('Carbon split and supplied glycolytic bookkeeping',[(40,170,190,100,['Glucose','6 carbons']),(355,170,190,100,['Cleavage','after 2 ATP used']),(670,110,190,100,['3-carbon branch','→ pyruvate']),(670,310,190,100,['3-carbon branch','→ pyruvate'])],[(0,1,''),(1,2,''),(1,3,'')],['Each branch forms 2 ATP and 1 reduced NAD.']),
[ck('Carbonconserved',2*3,6),ck('GrossATP',2*2,4),ck('NetATP',2*2-2,2),ck('ReducedNAD',2*1,2)],'Detailed glycolytic NAD and substrate-level ATP bookkeeping are AHL. No glycolytic intermediate names or universal aerobic ATP yield are demanded.','2.00 mmol pyruvate, 4.00 gross ATP, 2.00 net ATP and 2.00 reduced NAD per mmol glucose.')

add(726,'Fermentation restores an electron acceptor rather than adding ATP','C1.2','C1.2.8–10',
'A sealed cytoplasmic system starts with glucose, glycolytic enzymes, ADP/phosphate and a total NAD pool of 0.200 mmol, all initially oxidized. Each glucose converted to two pyruvates reduces two NAD. If reduced NAD is not reoxidized, glycolysis stops when all NAD is reduced. The system first lacks a fermentation enzyme; a lactate-producing enzyme is then added. This enzyme transfers reducing power from reduced NAD to pyruvate and forms no ATP directly.',
[P('Find the maximum glucose amount processed before NAD exhaustion in the initial system.',M('Two NAD are reduced per glucose, so glucose limit = 0.200/2.'),A('Maximum glucose processed = 0.100 mmol, if all initial NAD is oxidized.')),
 P('Explain why lactate production permits continued glycolytic ATP generation without oxygen.',R('Reduction of pyruvate to lactate reoxidizes reduced NAD to NAD.'),R('The regenerated NAD can accept hydrogen/electrons in glycolysis again, so glycolysis can continue making its net ATP.'),R('The lactate step itself gives no extra ATP; its role is coenzyme recycling rather than complete oxidation of pyruvate.')),
 P('Compare this with anaerobic respiration in yeast and predict carbon-containing products from 0.500 mmol glucose in each pathway.',A('The lactate pathway yields 1.00 mmol lactate without releasing carbon as CO2 in this conversion.'),A('The yeast pathway yields 1.00 mmol ethanol and 1.00 mmol CO2; pyruvate is decarboxylated before the remaining 2-carbon compound is reduced.'),R('Both pathways regenerate NAD, while the different final carbon products retain energy that has not been completely released.'))],
pathway('A finite coenzyme pool can be recycled',[(50,130,210,100,['Glycolysis','NAD → reduced NAD']),(600,130,230,100,['Pyruvate reduction','reduced NAD → NAD']),(320,340,240,100,['Shared NAD pool','total 0.200 mmol'])],[(0,1,'pyruvate'),(2,0,''),(1,2,'')]),
[ck('Glucoselimit',.2/2,.1),ck('Lactatemmol',.5*2,1),ck('Yeastcarbonmmolatoms',1*2+1*1,.5*6)],'AHL NAD recycling is distinguished from direct ATP synthesis. Lactate and ethanol/CO2 carbon balances are correct; anaerobic yeast is not assigned a mitochondrial requirement.','Initial glucose limit 0.100 mmol; fermentation restores NAD and allows glycolytic net ATP generation.')

add(727,'Acetyl input is balanced by carbon loss while an acceptor returns','C1.2','C1.2.11–12',
'In an aerobic mitochondrial model, each pyruvate loses one carbon as CO2 during the link reaction, producing a 2-carbon acetyl group and one reduced NAD. One Krebs-cycle turn joins this acetyl group to 4-carbon oxaloacetate, forms 6-carbon citrate, releases two CO2 and regenerates oxaloacetate. Per turn the supplied accounting is three reduced NAD, one other reduced carrier and one ATP equivalent. Do not infer which specific acetyl carbons leave in the first turn.',
[P('For 1.50 mmol pyruvate entering the link reaction, calculate CO2 released by link plus complete cycle turns and the total reduced NAD formed in these two stages.',M('There are 1.50 mmol link reactions and 1.50 mmol cycle turns.'),A('CO2 = 1.50×(1+2) = 4.50 mmol.'),A('Reduced NAD = 1.50×(1+3) = 6.00 mmol.')),
 P('Explain why oxaloacetate is required but is not consumed once per cycle turn.',R('Oxaloacetate accepts the 2-carbon acetyl group to form citrate at the start.'),R('Later cycle reactions regenerate the 4-carbon acceptor, permitting another acetyl group to enter.')),
 P('Evaluate two conclusions about the carbon and energy accounting.',R('Carbon numbers balance through 4+2=6 followed by loss of two CO2 and regeneration of a 4-carbon molecule.'),R('The two CO2 in a turn are not sufficient evidence that the newly entered acetyl carbon atoms were both released in that same turn; positional tracing would be needed.'),R('Much energy is transferred to reduced carriers rather than all being captured directly as the one ATP equivalent; these electrons can feed later aerobic processes.'))],
svg(box(310,100,260,85,['Oxaloacetate: 4C'])+box(610,260,230,85,['Citrate: 6C'])+box(75,260,230,85,['Later cycle','regenerates 4C'])+arr(570,142,710,255)+arr(610,305,310,305)+arr(185,255,310,155)+text(730,175,'+ acetyl: 2C',19)+text(450,365,'2 CO2 released per turn',19)+text(450,460,'Schematic cycle; not a carbon-position tracer map.',18),'A regenerated carbon acceptor in the Krebs cycle'),
[ck('LinkpluscycleCO2',1.5*(1+2),4.5),ck('LinkpluscycleNADH',1.5*(1+3),6),ck('Cyclecarbonbalance',4+2-2,4)],'AHL link/Krebs carbon and redox accounting is used, with no extra intermediate names. The first-turn isotope caveat prevents a common carbon-identity error.','4.50 mmol CO2 and 6.00 mmol reduced NAD; the 4-carbon acceptor is regenerated.')

add(728,'Blocking ATP synthase differs from making the membrane leaky','C1.2','C1.2.13–16',
'The inner mitochondrial membrane schematic has intermembrane space above and matrix below. X moves protons upward using energy from electron transfer; Y allows protons downward and has its catalytic head below. In a supplied experiment, control oxygen uptake, ATP production and proton-gradient size are 10, 30 and 8 relative units. Blocking Y gives 2, 0 and 12; adding a proton leak to the blocked preparation gives 13, 0 and 1. Other substrates remain available.',
[P('Identify X and Y by function and explain where ATP is produced.',A('X represents electron-transfer-linked proton pumping; Y is ATP synthase.'),R('Protons are pumped from matrix to intermembrane space, then flow back down their electrochemical gradient through ATP synthase.'),R('The catalytic head faces the matrix, where ADP is phosphorylated to ATP.')),
 P('Explain why blocking Y increases the gradient but lowers oxygen uptake, and why the leak changes oxygen uptake without restoring ATP.',R('Blocking proton return prevents ATP synthesis and allows a steep gradient to accumulate, opposing further proton pumping/electron flow.'),R('A leak dissipates the gradient so electron flow and oxygen consumption can accelerate again.'),R('Proton return through a nonspecific leak bypasses the blocked synthase, so ATP synthesis is not restored.')),
 P('Calculate the control ATP/oxygen-unit ratio and evaluate its use outside this model.',M('Use 30/10.'),A('The control ratio is 3.00 supplied ATP units per supplied oxygen unit.'),R('These are relative experimental units under particular conditions, not a universal ATP yield per oxygen molecule or per glucose.'))],
membrane('Functional orientation of the inner mitochondrial membrane','Intermembrane space','Matrix','mitochondrion',('X','Y')),
[ck('ControlrelativeATPoxygen',30/10,3),yes('LeakuncouplesoxygenfromATP',13>10 and 0==0),yes('Blockedgradientsteeper',12>8)],'Mitochondrial orientation is matrix→space pumping and space→matrix synthase flow. Oxygen is the terminal acceptor; no ETC complex names or universal ATP yield are demanded.','X proton pump, Y ATP synthase; synthase block builds gradient, leak restores electron flow but not ATP.')

add(729,'Oxygen demand follows pairs of electrons accepted at the chain’s end','C1.2','C1.2.7, C1.2.13–16',
'In a mitochondrial preparation, 0.600 mmol reduced NAD is oxidized completely through an electron transport chain. Each reduced NAD supplies one pair of electrons. The terminal reaction is O2+4e−+4H+→2H2O. Ignore other electron donors. A separate preparation lacks oxygen but starts with the same amount of oxidized NAD and substrate.',
[P('Calculate electron amount, minimum oxygen consumption and water formation for complete oxidation of the reduced NAD.',M('0.600 mmol reduced NAD supplies 1.20 mmol electrons.'),A('O2 required = 1.20/4 = 0.300 mmol.'),A('Water formed = 2×0.300 = 0.600 mmol.')),
 P('Explain why oxygen removal can slow the Krebs cycle even though oxygen is not a reactant in each cycle step.',R('Without the terminal electron acceptor, electron carriers cannot continue transferring electrons at the same rate.'),R('Reduced NAD is not reoxidized sufficiently, so oxidized NAD becomes limiting for dehydrogenation reactions in the link reaction and Krebs cycle.'),R('This is indirect coupling through the redox carrier pool rather than oxygen being incorporated at every cycle step.')),
 P('Evaluate the claim that adding more ADP alone solves the oxygen-free limitation.',R('More ADP supplies a phosphorylation substrate but does not provide the missing terminal electron acceptor.'),R('An alternative NAD-regenerating fermentation route could support glycolysis in suitable cells, but would not restore this oxygen-dependent mitochondrial electron flow.'))],
pathway('Reducing power flows toward a terminal acceptor',[(40,175,220,120,['Reduced NAD','one electron pair']),(350,175,220,120,['Electron carriers','proton pumping']),(665,175,200,120,['Terminal reaction','O2 → H2O'])],[(0,1,'electrons'),(1,2,'electrons')]),
[ck('Electronsmmol',.6*2,1.2),ck('Oxygenmmol',.6*2/4,.3),ck('Watermmol',.6*2/4*2,.6)],'Electron stoichiometry and NAD regeneration are AHL. Oxygen’s indirect effect on NAD-dependent steps is distinguished from direct cycle-substrate use.','1.20 mmol electrons require 0.300 mmol O2 and form 0.600 mmol water.')

add(730,'A respiratory quotient can estimate a supplied substrate mixture','C1.2','C1.2.16–17',
'For this constructed substrate-mixture model, complete oxidation of carbohydrate consumes 1 mol O2 per mol CO2 produced, whereas the chosen lipid substrate produces 0.700 mol CO2 per mol O2. Let f be the fraction of total oxygen uptake attributable to carbohydrate; then mixture RQ=CO2/O2=f(1.00)+(1−f)(0.700). Measured rates are 0.850 mmol CO2 min−1 and 1.00 mmol O2 min−1. These relationships and the linear oxygen-weighting assumption are supplied.',
[P('Calculate RQ and f, clearly stating what the resulting fraction measures.',M('RQ=0.850/1.00=0.850; solve 0.850=0.700+0.300f.'),A('f=0.500; 50.0% of oxygen uptake is assigned to carbohydrate oxidation in this model.')),
 P('Explain why the fraction is not necessarily 50% of substrate mass or ATP production.',R('The model weights by oxygen uptake; different substrates consume different amounts of oxygen per gram or molecule.'),R('ATP yield depends on the pathway and coupling efficiency, which are not specified as equal for the two substrates.')),
 P('Explain the aerobic roles of carbon compounds and oxygen, and evaluate one assumption of the mixture estimate.',R('Carbon substrates are oxidized and transfer reducing power to carriers; oxygen accepts electrons at the end of the respiratory electron-transfer process.'),R('Lipids as well as carbohydrates can provide substrates for aerobic respiration after conversion into pathway inputs.'),R('Incomplete oxidation, changing body CO2 stores or another substrate could break the supplied two-component relationship; gas exchange alone does not uniquely identify every substrate without the stated assumptions.'))],
graph('Supplied two-substrate oxygen-weighted model',[('Model',[(0,.7),(.5,.85),(1,1)])],(0,1),(.65,1.05),'Fraction of O2 used for carbohydrate, f','Respiratory quotient, CO2/O2'),
[ck('MeasuredRQ',.85/1,.85),ck('Oxygenweightedfraction',(.85-.7)/(.1e1-.7),.5)],'RQ formula and mixture weighting are supplied extensions. AHL substrate use and terminal oxygen acceptance are tested; oxygen-weighted fractions are not equated with mass or ATP fractions.','RQ 0.850; f=0.500 of oxygen uptake, with no justified equal mass or ATP contribution.')

add(731,'An artificial thylakoid gradient can drive ATP synthesis in darkness','C1.3','C1.3.10–14',
'The schematic represents a sealed thylakoid membrane. Stroma is above and lumen below. X uses light-driven electron transfer to move H+ into the lumen; Y lets H+ return to the stroma and has its catalytic head facing the stroma. In an artificial experiment, lumen pH is 5.00 and external stroma-side pH is 8.00 before light is removed. ADP and phosphate are available on the stroma side. For the supplied device only, 3 H+ through Y are required per ATP and 120 nmol H+ can flow before the gradient dissipates. Ignore electrical potential when calculating the concentration ratio.',
[P('Calculate the initial lumen/stroma proton concentration ratio and the maximum ATP from the supplied proton amount.',M('The ratio is 10^(−5)/10^(−8)=10³; ATP amount is 120/3.'),A('Lumen [H+] is 1000 times stroma [H+]; maximum ATP = 40.0 nmol.')),
 P('Explain why ATP can briefly form in darkness and identify X and Y.',A('X is light-driven proton pumping linked to electron transport; Y is ATP synthase.'),R('The stored electrochemical proton gradient provides the driving force for H+ movement through ATP synthase; light is not directly used by the catalytic head.'),R('ATP formation can continue briefly until the gradient dissipates, but cannot be sustained without restoring a gradient.')),
 P('Predict the effect of a membrane proton leak and explain the importance of Y’s orientation.',R('A leak lets H+ return without passing through ATP synthase, reducing the ATP yield from the stored gradient.'),R('The catalytic head must face the stroma-side ADP/phosphate pool in this preparation; reversing orientation changes access and the direction of coupled synthesis.'),R('The stated 3 H+/ATP belongs to this constructed device and is not asserted as a universal intact-chloroplast stoichiometry.'))],
membrane('Proton pumping and synthase orientation in a thylakoid','Stroma','Thylakoid lumen','chloroplast',('X','Y')),
[ck('Protonratio',10**-5/10**-8,1000),ck('MaximumATPnmol',120/3,40)],'Thylakoid pumping is stroma→lumen; synthase return is lumen→stroma with head in stroma. Concentration ratio ignores voltage by instruction; proton/ATP bookkeeping is supplied, not universal.','Proton ratio 1000; maximum 40.0 nmol ATP; stored gradient can support a brief dark synthesis burst.')

add(732,'Cyclic electron flow can meet an ATP shortfall without making NADPH','C1.3','C1.3.10–13, C1.3.16–18',
'For a constructed chloroplast model, fixing 3 CO2 into one net triose phosphate requires 9 ATP and 6 reduced NADP. A non-cyclic electron-flow run makes 6 reduced NADP and 6 ATP. An added cyclic run returns electrons to the transfer chain and makes ATP but no net reduced NADP or oxygen. These numerical yields are supplied for this preparation; they are not universal photon or proton yields.',
[P('Calculate the ATP shortfall and the ATP:reduced-NADP ratio needed by carbon fixation versus that supplied by the non-cyclic run.',M('ATP shortfall = 9−6; required ratio = 9/6; supplied ratio = 6/6.'),A('Shortfall = 3 ATP units; required ratio 1.50, supplied ratio 1.00.')),
 P('Explain how cyclic electron flow can address this mismatch.',R('Excited electrons from photosystem I return to the electron-transfer pathway rather than reducing NADP.'),R('Electron flow supports proton-gradient formation and ATP synthesis without net NADP reduction.'),R('It can add the missing ATP without adding more reducing power than the stated carbon-fixation demand.')),
 P('Compare the source of electrons and oxygen production in non-cyclic flow with cyclic flow.',R('Non-cyclic flow obtains replacement electrons by splitting water at photosystem II; oxygen is a product of that water oxidation.'),R('Non-cyclic electrons eventually reduce NADP after excitation at photosystem I, rather than returning to the same route.'),R('Cyclic flow alone does not split water or generate net oxygen; its ATP output cannot be inferred from oxygen evolution alone.'))],
svg(box(65,160,210,100,['Photosystem II','water supplies e−'])+box(350,160,210,100,['Transfer chain','proton pumping'])+box(645,160,210,100,['Photosystem I','excitation'])+arr(275,210,350,210)+arr(560,210,645,210)+arr(750,275,750,370)+arr(750,370,455,370)+arr(455,370,455,275)+text(600,410,'Cyclic return route',20)+arr(750,145,750,85)+text(750,65,'NADP reduction route',18),'Alternative destinations of excited electrons'),
[ck('ATPshortfall',9-6,3),ck('Demandratio',9/6,1.5),ck('Supplyratio',6/6,1)],'Actual AHL cyclic/non-cyclic mechanisms are required. Supplied preparation yields avoid universal ATP/photon claims; cyclic flow is not drawn producing oxygen or net reduced NADP.','3 extra ATP units are needed; cyclic PSI electron flow can add ATP without net NADPH or oxygen.')

add(733,'The Calvin cycle exports one carbon product while regenerating its acceptor','C1.3','C1.3.15–18',
'A Calvin-cycle model fixes 3 CO2 using 3 RuBP molecules, each containing 5 carbons. The resulting unstable products give 6 GP molecules, each with 3 carbons. Reduction forms 6 TP molecules, each with 3 carbons. One TP is exported and the other five regenerate the 3 RuBP. For this model, reduction uses 6 ATP and 6 reduced NADP; regeneration uses 3 further ATP.',
[P('Check carbon conservation through fixation, reduction/export and regeneration.',M('Initial carbon = 3×5+3×1=18; six GP and six TP each contain 6×3=18 carbons.'),A('Five retained TP contain 15 carbons, exactly enough for three 5-carbon RuBP.'),A('One exported TP contains the three newly fixed carbons in the net balance.')),
 P('Calculate ATP and reduced NADP required for a net pair of exported TP, and explain why the model has net gain despite being cyclic.',M('Two net TP require two sets of the supplied 3-CO2 operation.'),A('ATP = 2×(6+3)=18; reduced NADP = 2×6=12.'),R('The acceptor is regenerated, while extra carbon enters as CO2 and some carbon leaves as product; cyclic acceptor regeneration does not mean zero net input/output.')),
 P('Explain the roles of rubisco and the light-dependent reactions.',R('Rubisco catalyses fixation of CO2 by reaction with RuBP; the products form GP.'),R('Light-dependent processes supply ATP and reduced NADP for GP reduction and RuBP regeneration; they do not supply CO2 carbon.'),R('GP and TP are different oxidation states, so ATP alone without reducing power cannot complete the stated conversion.'))],
pathway('Carbon-number bookkeeping in the Calvin cycle',[(45,120,215,100,['3 RuBP','15 carbon atoms']),(345,120,215,100,['6 GP','18 carbon atoms']),(650,120,215,100,['6 TP','18 carbon atoms']),(345,340,215,100,['5 TP retained','1 TP exported'])],[(0,1,'+ 3 CO2'),(1,2,'ATP + reduced NADP'),(2,3,''),(3,0,'regeneration')],['Five TP regenerate three RuBP; the diagram does not track carbon positions.']),
[ck('Fixationcarbon',3*5+3,6*3),ck('Regenerationcarbon',5*3,3*5),ck('TwoTPATP',2*(6+3),18),ck('TwoTPNADPH',2*6,12)],'RuBP/GP/TP and rubisco are required AHL intermediates; no detailed regeneration reactions are demanded. Net carbon accounting is not a positional isotope claim.','18 carbons conserved; two net TP require 18 ATP and 12 reduced NADP in the supplied model.')

add(734,'A dark transition reveals which Calvin intermediate accumulates','C1.3','C1.3.16–19',
'An illuminated chloroplast model reaches a steady state, then light is switched off at t=0 while CO2 remains available. At t=0, 5, 15 and 30 s, GP amounts are 20, 35, 50 and 50 nmol, while TP amounts are 20, 14, 7 and 4 nmol. ATP and reduced NADP rapidly decline. The early interval allows some residual RuBP fixation, but does not assume infinite RuBP stores.',
[P('Calculate the GP:TP ratio before the change and at 30 s and describe the paired changes.',M('Use 20/20 and 50/4.'),A('The ratio rises from 1.00 to 12.5.'),A('GP increases then plateaus, whereas TP falls throughout the sampled period.')),
 P('Explain the early accumulation of GP and falling TP.',R('Loss of light stops sustained ATP/reduced-NADP supply, limiting conversion of GP to TP.'),R('Residual RuBP can still be fixed briefly, adding GP while its reduction is slowed.'),R('Existing TP continues to be used/exported or redirected into regeneration, so its pool can decline.')),
 P('Explain the GP plateau and evaluate a proposed rescue using CO2 alone.',R('Without sufficient ATP-supported regeneration, RuBP becomes limiting, so continued GP production cannot be sustained indefinitely.'),R('Extra CO2 alone does not replace ATP or reducing power, so it cannot restore the complete light-coupled cycle.'),R('Pool sizes show the balance of formation and consumption, not a direct measurement of every reaction flux; use additional time-resolved ATP/RuBP measurements to test the explanation.'))],
graph('Constructed intermediate pools after lights off',[('GP',[(0,20),(5,35),(15,50),(30,50)]),('TP',[(0,20),(5,14),(15,7),(30,4)])],(0,30),(0,60),'Time after dark transition / s','Intermediate pool / nmol'),
[ck('InitialGPratio',20/20,1),ck('FinalGPratio',50/4,12.5)],'The light/Calvin interdependence is AHL. A finite RuBP pool prevents a false claim of indefinite GP formation in darkness; pool amount is distinct from flux.','GP:TP rises 1.00→12.5; lost ATP/reducing power slows GP reduction and later RuBP regeneration.')

add(735,'A selective electron donor tests a photosystem block','C1.3','C1.3.9–13, C1.3.19',
'A constructed preparation has two light-absorbing photosystems in sequence. A treatment prevents water-derived electrons entering photosystem II but leaves photosystem I and its downstream NADP reduction machinery intact. Under illumination, oxygen evolution falls from 8 to 0 units and reduced-NADP production from 16 to 0. A supplied artificial donor that gives electrons directly to photosystem I restores reduced-NADP production to 10 units, while oxygen remains zero. The donor’s chemistry need not be recalled.',
[P('Calculate the fraction of control reduced-NADP production restored by the donor and explain why oxygen is not restored.',M('Use 10/16×100.'),A('Reduced-NADP production is restored to 62.5% of control.'),R('The artificial donor bypasses the blocked water/PSII entry; it does not split water, so it does not restore the source of oxygen.')),
 P('Explain what the rescue establishes about the remaining light-dependent pathway.',R('Photosystem I can still use light excitation to support downstream reduction of NADP when it receives replacement electrons.'),R('The original loss of reduced NADP is therefore consistent with lack of electron supply, not necessarily destruction of every downstream component.')),
 P('Evaluate two claims about ATP and carbon fixation from these results.',R('Restored reduced NADP alone does not quantify ATP formation; electron routing and proton movement also need measurement.'),R('Calvin-cycle carbon fixation needs ATP as well as reduced NADP, so 62.5% reducing-power rescue does not prove 62.5% carbon-fixation rescue.'),R('Confirm selectivity with independent controls for light absorption, donor effects and component integrity; rescue is strong pathway evidence but is conditional on the stated bypass.'))],
pathway('Supplied bypass of a water-electron entry block',[(35,180,170,105,['Water','entry blocked']),(310,180,170,105,['Photosystem II']),(590,180,175,105,['Photosystem I']),(590,355,240,95,['Artificial donor','electrons to PSI'])],[(0,1,'X'),(1,2,''),(3,2,'')],['Downstream PSI output can reduce NADP; oxygen comes from water splitting.']),
[ck('NADPHrescuepercent',10/16*100,62.5),ck('Oxygenrescue',0/8,0)],'PSII photolysis and PSI NADP reduction are actual AHL requirements. Donor action is supplied; oxygen evolution is not treated as a direct ATP assay.','62.5% reducing-power rescue; no O2 rescue because artificial electron donation does not split water.')

add(736,'Conditioned medium separates bacterial density from its signal','C2.1','C2.1.1–2, C2.1.5, C2.1.7',
'Vibrio fischeri is used in a constructed quorum-sensing investigation. Low-density cells emit 2 light units per 10⁶ cells. Adding sterile-filtered medium from a high-density culture gives 40 units, while fresh medium gives 3. A signal-degrading treatment applied to conditioned medium reduces the effect to 4 units; heat-inactivated degrading treatment gives 38. Cell numbers, nutrients and oxygen are matched during the short assay.',
[P('Calculate the fold response to untreated conditioned medium relative to low-density cells and compare the two degrading treatments.',M('Use 40/2 for fold change.'),A('Conditioned medium gives a 20.0-fold response; active degradation gives 4 units versus 38 for heat-inactivated degradation.')),
 P('Explain how the results support quorum sensing through a diffusible ligand.',R('A high-density culture can accumulate a signalling chemical that is sensed by specific receptors.'),R('Cell-free conditioned medium transfers the effect, so cell contact or high cell number in the assay is not necessary for this short response.'),R('Loss of effect after active signal degradation, with retained effect after inactivated treatment, supports a chemical signal rather than merely the added treatment solution.')),
 P('Evaluate what is and is not demonstrated.',R('Matching nutrients, cell numbers and oxygen reduces major alternative explanations for changes in bioluminescence.'),R('The data do not alone identify the ligand or receptor; purification and receptor-disruption/rescue experiments would test that mechanism further.'),R('Normalization per 10⁶ cells avoids mistaking more cells for more expression per cell; independent cultures are still needed to estimate variability.'))],
bars('Bioluminescence at matched low cell density',['Low density','Conditioned','Fresh medium','Degraded','Inactive|degrader'],[2,40,3,4,38],'Light units per 10⁶ cells',50),
[ck('Conditionedfold',40/2,20),ck('Activeinactiveeffectratio',4/38,.105263157895)],'Entire C2.1 is AHL. Vibrio fischeri quorum sensing is current scope; medium-transfer and control evidence does not uniquely identify the molecular receptor.','Conditioned medium gives 20.0-fold light response; signal degradation suppresses the transferable effect.')

receptorbody='<rect x="70" y="205" width="760" height="65" fill="#eee" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'
receptorbody+=box(220,155,125,165,['R','membrane'])+circle(282,115,23)+arr(282,142,282,154)
receptorbody+=circle(650,120,20)+arr(650,145,650,340)+box(590,350,125,80,['S receptor'])+text(105,145,'Outside',20)+text(105,335,'Cytoplasm',20)+text(282,80,'Ligand P',19)+text(650,80,'Ligand S',19)+text(450,485,'P is hydrophilic; S is a steroid. Arrows show routes in the supplied model.',18)
add(737,'Receptor location must match how a ligand crosses a membrane','C2.1','C2.1.1, C2.1.4–7, C2.1.12',
'In a constructed animal-cell model, hydrophilic protein ligand P remains outside intact cells and binds receptor R in the plasma membrane. Steroid S enters the cell and binds an intracellular receptor. Introducing the S receptor into a previously unresponsive cell gives an S-dependent increase in a target transcript, provided its DNA response sequence is intact. Cells with a mutated response sequence bind S but do not increase this transcript.',
[P('Explain the different receptor locations and the orientation of R.',R('Hydrophilic P does not readily cross the hydrophobic bilayer, so its binding site must be exposed extracellularly on R.'),R('The membrane-spanning portion of R has hydrophobic surfaces compatible with the bilayer, while aqueous exposed regions are relatively hydrophilic.'),R('Steroid S can cross the bilayer and bind a receptor in cytoplasm or nucleus.')),
 P('Deduce why S binding is not sufficient for this transcript response.',R('The activated intracellular receptor must interact with a specific DNA sequence to regulate transcription.'),R('The mutated sequence allows ligand binding but removes the downstream DNA-dependent response in this model.')),
 P('Evaluate the receptor-introduction experiment as evidence of target-cell specificity.',R('Gaining a response after receptor introduction supports receptor availability as one determinant of whether a cell is a target.'),R('The DNA-sequence result shows that a receptor is not sufficient without compatible downstream machinery.'),R('Include empty-vector controls and matched receptor expression/cell viability; introduced receptors can differ from normal physiological amounts.'))],
svg(receptorbody,'Ligand permeability and receptor orientation'),
[yes('Membrane receptor crosses both membrane faces',155<205 and 155+165>270),yes('Intracellular receptor remains cytoplasmic',350>270)],'C2.1 is AHL. The hydrophilic ligand-binding side is extracellular, membrane span hydrophobic, and steroid response requires receptor plus DNA-dependent machinery.','P uses an extracellular membrane-receptor site; steroid S uses an intracellular receptor and compatible DNA response sequence.')

add(738,'Bypass rescue orders an adrenaline signalling pathway','C2.1','C2.1.7, C2.1.9–10, C2.1.14',
'A constructed liver-cell pathway links adrenaline receptor binding to a G protein, production of cAMP and a measured response. Response units are: adrenaline alone, 100; adrenaline plus receptor blocker, 8; receptor blocker plus membrane-permeable cAMP analogue, 92; adrenaline plus inhibitor of an enzyme downstream of cAMP, 6; downstream inhibitor plus cAMP analogue, 7. Assume the blockers have their stated selective targets. The cAMP analogue acts like intracellular cAMP.',
[P('Order receptor, G protein/cAMP generation and the blocked downstream enzyme using the rescue results.',R('The receptor acts upstream of cAMP because adding a cAMP analogue largely bypasses receptor blockade.'),R('The second blocked enzyme acts downstream of cAMP because adding the analogue does not rescue its inhibition.'),A('Supported order: adrenaline receptor → G protein/cAMP generation → downstream enzyme → response.')),
 P('Calculate response recovery after receptor blockade with cAMP analogue, relative to the adrenaline control, and explain second-messenger action.',M('Recovery relative to control = 92/100×100.'),A('Recovery = 92.0% of the control response.'),R('Adrenaline is the extracellular first messenger; intracellular cAMP relays the receptor-triggered signal to downstream targets.')),
 P('Explain how response termination could occur without permanently destroying the receptor and evaluate a limit of the pathway inference.',R('Removing adrenaline and degrading cAMP or reversing downstream activation can terminate signalling.'),R('The ordering depends on selective inhibition and analogue access; off-target toxicity or unequal delivery could create misleading failure of rescue.'))],
bars('Responses used to order the signalling pathway',['Adrenaline','Receptor|blocked','R blocked|+ cAMP','Downstream|blocked','D blocked|+ cAMP'],[100,8,92,6,7],'Response / relative units',120),
[ck('Recoverypercent',92/100*100,92),yes('Receptorblockrescued',92>8),yes('Downstreamblocknotrescued',7<10)],'Adrenaline/G protein/cAMP and pathway ordering are AHL. Bypass inference is conditional on explicitly stated selectivity; no enzyme-name recall beyond the guide is required.','Receptor → G protein/cAMP → downstream enzyme; cAMP analogue restores 92.0% after receptor blockade only.')

ins='<rect x="70" y="200" width="760" height="65" fill="#eee" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'
ins+=box(350,140,200,190,['Insulin receptor','inside tyrosine sites'])+circle(450,95,25)+arr(450,122,450,140)+text(450,60,'Insulin outside cell',19)
ins+=circle(220,380,58)+text(220,385,'Vesicle',17)+arr(220,320,220,275)+text(620,355,'ATP → ADP',19)+text(620,390,'intracellular phosphorylation',17)+text(450,480,'Transporter-containing vesicles can join the plasma membrane.',19)
add(739,'Insulin binding and intracellular phosphorylation are separate steps','C2.1','C2.1.7, C2.1.11',
'In a constructed muscle-cell model, insulin binds normally to wild-type and mutant receptors. After insulin treatment, wild-type cells show receptor-associated tyrosine phosphorylation and 80 surface glucose transporters per measured membrane patch. Mutant cells lack this phosphorylation and show 20, the untreated baseline. An experimental downstream activation bypass restores 72 surface transporters in mutant cells. Total cellular transporter number is unchanged during the short assay.',
[P('Explain how insulin reception normally increases glucose transport capacity.',R('Insulin binds the extracellular portion of a receptor in the plasma membrane.'),R('Binding activates intracellular tyrosine phosphorylation and a downstream signalling sequence.'),R('Transporter-containing vesicles move to and fuse with the plasma membrane, increasing available surface glucose transporters.')),
 P('Calculate the fold surface increase in wild type and the fraction of the insulin-induced increase rescued by the bypass.',M('Wild-type fold = 80/20; rescued increase fraction = (72−20)/(80−20).'),A('Wild-type surface count rises 4.00-fold; 86.7% of the induced increase is rescued.')),
 P('Deduce a location for the mutant defect and evaluate the result.',R('Normal binding but absent phosphorylation places the defect after ligand binding and at/before receptor-associated kinase signalling.'),R('Downstream bypass rescue shows that the vesicle/transporter machinery retains substantial function.'),R('Surface redistribution, not rapid synthesis of new transporter protein, explains unchanged total count; the model does not prove every possible downstream target is normal.'))],
svg(ins,'Insulin receptor orientation and transporter-vesicle response'),
[ck('Surfacefold',80/20,4),ck('Baselinecorrectedrescuepercent',(72-20)/(80-20)*100,86.6666666667)],'Current AHL insulin-receptor scope is limited to intracellular tyrosine phosphorylation and transporter-vesicle movement. Baseline-corrected rescue is distinguished from raw percentage.','Fourfold surface increase; bypass restores 86.7% of the induced increase, placing the defect near receptor signalling.')

add(740,'Oestradiol feedback depends on concentration and target-cell context','C2.1','C2.1.12–14',
'A supplied model concerns oestradiol-responsive GnRH-secreting hypothalamic cells. GnRH promotes a hormonal cascade that increases ovarian oestradiol. Relative GnRH output after equal exposure is 100 without oestradiol, 60 at a moderate sustained concentration and 150 at a high sustained concentration. Receptor-blocked cells remain near 100 in both hormone treatments. The model explicitly supplies a switch from negative to positive feedback at high sustained oestradiol; it does not assume that every tissue has this response.',
[P('Calculate the percentage changes in GnRH output at the two concentrations.',M('Use (treated−100)/100×100.'),A('Moderate concentration: −40.0%; high sustained concentration: +50.0%.')),
 P('Explain receptor-dependent gene regulation and distinguish the two feedback signs.',R('Oestradiol enters the cell and activates an intracellular receptor that can bind specific DNA sequences and alter transcription.'),R('In the supplied moderate-concentration model, reduced upstream GnRH output opposes further stimulation: negative feedback.'),R('In the supplied high sustained condition, increased GnRH output amplifies upstream stimulation: positive feedback.')),
 P('Evaluate why these data cannot be generalized to all steroid targets or attributed only to extracellular receptor binding.',R('The demonstrated target is the hypothalamic GnRH cell, and response also depends on receptor expression and downstream gene-regulatory context.'),R('Oestradiol is a steroid capable of membrane entry; intracellular receptor action is consistent with the stated gene-regulatory mechanism.'),R('Receptor blockade supports dependence on the receptor, but independent controls for blocker specificity and cell viability are needed before excluding other causes.'))],
bars('Supplied GnRH-cell response to sustained oestradiol',['None','Moderate','High sustained'],[100,60,150],'Relative GnRH output',200),
[ck('Moderatepercent',(60-100)/100*100,-40),ck('Highpercent',(150-100)/100*100,50)],'Oestradiol effects are confined to GnRH-secreting hypothalamic cells as required. The biphasic relation is supplied model data rather than an unsupported universal steroid response.','GnRH −40.0% versus +50.0%; feedback sign changes in the explicitly supplied receptor-dependent model.')

add(741,'Progesterone changes transcription before a secretory response','C2.1','C2.1.12–13',
'A constructed endometrial-cell model measures production of a new target transcript and its encoded secreted protein after progesterone is added. At 0, 1, 3 and 6 h, transcript-production rates are 3, 9, 12 and 12 units, while accumulated secreted protein is 0, 1, 7 and 20 units. A selective block of receptor–DNA binding prevents the transcript increase but does not alter progesterone entry. Cell number and viability are matched.',
[P('Calculate the maximum transcript-rate fold increase and compare the timing of transcript and secreted-protein responses.',M('Use 12/3.'),A('Maximum transcript-production rate is 4.00 times baseline.'),A('Transcript rate rises by 1 h and reaches a plateau by 3 h, while secreted protein continues to accumulate to 6 h.')),
 P('Explain a mechanism connecting progesterone to the target transcript.',R('Progesterone enters the endometrial cell and binds its intracellular receptor.'),R('The activated receptor binds a specific DNA sequence and regulates transcription of a target gene.'),R('Blocking receptor–DNA binding can therefore prevent increased transcription even when hormone entry remains normal.')),
 P('Evaluate whether the two response variables measure the same quantity and identify a stronger causal test of the protein link.',R('Transcript-production rate is a rate, whereas secreted protein is an accumulated amount; their numerical values cannot be compared as if they were the same units.'),R('Protein accumulation also depends on translation, secretion and degradation, so a transcription plateau does not require a protein-amount plateau.'),R('Selectively inhibit production of the target transcript or its translation, with specificity/viability controls, and test whether the later protein response is lost and can be rescued.'))],
graph('Time course of newly secreted protein',[('Secreted protein',[(0,0),(1,1),(3,7),(6,20)])],(0,6),(0,24),'Time after progesterone / h','Accumulated secreted protein / relative units'),
[ck('Transcriptfold',12/3,4),yes('Proteincontinuesaftertranscriptplateau',20>7 and 12==12)],'Progesterone target is endometrium. Rate versus cumulative amount is explicitly distinguished; correlation of transcript and protein is not treated as full causal proof.','Transcript rate rises fourfold; intracellular receptor–DNA action precedes a cumulative protein response.')

add(742,'Persistent ligand binding can coexist with feedback adaptation','C2.1','C2.1.7, C2.1.9–10, C2.1.14',
'In a constructed receptor/cAMP pathway, a constant adrenaline treatment keeps receptor occupancy near 80%. cAMP is 1 unit before treatment, 10 at 1 min, 4 at 4 min and 2 at 8 min. Disabling a specified negative-feedback step leaves occupancy unchanged but cAMP is still 10 units at 8 min. The feedback step normally suppresses cAMP generation downstream of receptor activation. A separate supplied amplification model has one active receptor activating 20 enzymes, each generating 100 cAMP molecules in the measured interval.',
[P('Calculate the peak cAMP fold change, the percentage decrease from peak to 8 min with feedback and the cAMP output of the amplification model.',M('Use 10/1, (10−2)/10×100 and 20×100.'),A('Peak cAMP is 10.0-fold baseline and falls 80.0% from peak.'),A('The supplied amplification model generates 2000 cAMP molecules per active receptor in the interval.')),
 P('Explain why adaptation does not require loss of ligand binding and why the intervention supports feedback.',R('Receptor occupancy stays high while cAMP declines, so dissociation of all ligand is not required for the response to weaken.'),R('Disabling the stated downstream feedback preserves the high cAMP level, supporting a causal role for feedback suppression in this model.')),
 P('Distinguish amplification from positive feedback and propose one measurement needed to assess specificity.',R('Amplification means one upstream event produces many downstream molecules; it need not feed back to increase its own initiating activity.'),R('Positive feedback specifically reinforces the upstream process, whereas this feedback suppresses cAMP generation.'),R('Measure cAMP production/degradation and cell viability under the intervention, using an appropriate inactive-treatment control, to check whether its effect matches the stated selective mechanism.'))],
graph('Adaptation despite maintained receptor occupancy',[('Feedback intact',[(0,1),(1,10),(4,4),(8,2)]),('Feedback disabled',[(0,1),(1,10),(4,10),(8,10)])],(0,8),(0,12),'Time after adrenaline / min','Intracellular cAMP / relative units'),
[ck('Peakfold',10/1,10),ck('Adaptationpercent',(10-2)/10*100,80),ck('Amplificationmolecules',20*100,2000)],'Signal amplification is distinct from feedback sign. The selective negative-feedback mechanism is supplied, with constant receptor occupancy preventing an unsupported dissociation explanation.','10-fold peak, 80.0% adaptation and 2000 cAMP molecules in the amplification model; amplification is not automatically positive feedback.')

add(743,'Voltage-gated events explain a spike without using the pump as a reset switch','C2.2','C2.2.8–10, C2.2.13',
'A schematic membrane-potential trace has values −70, −55, +30, −70, −80 and −70 mV at 0, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0 and 5.0 ms. Threshold is −55 mV. A selective voltage-gated Na+ channel blocker prevents the rapid rising phase without directly blocking the sodium–potassium pump. All values are relative to the outside.',
[P('Calculate the change from rest to peak and identify the phases from 0.5 to 1.0 ms and 1.0 to 2.0 ms.',M('Peak−rest = 30−(−70).'),A('The change is +100 mV; 0.5–1.0 ms is rapid depolarization and 1.0–2.0 ms is repolarization.')),
 P('Explain the main ion movements responsible for those phases and the undershoot.',R('Threshold activation opens voltage-gated Na+ channels, allowing Na+ influx down its electrochemical gradient.'),R('Na+ channels inactivate and voltage-gated K+ channels allow K+ efflux, repolarizing the membrane.'),R('Some K+ channels remain open briefly, causing the membrane to become more negative than rest before it returns.')),
 P('Distinguish the pump’s role and explain why immediately re-exciting the membrane is limited.',R('The sodium–potassium pump maintains ion gradients over time; it is not the direct rapid mechanism that produces each repolarizing phase.'),R('Recently inactivated Na+ channels cannot immediately reopen, creating a refractory interval.'),R('The blocker evidence supports Na+-channel involvement in the rising phase, but does not by itself quantify the pump’s long-term contribution.'))],
graph('Schematic action potential recorded at one membrane patch',[('Potential',[(0,-70),(.5,-55),(1,30),(2,-70),(3,-80),(5,-70)])],(0,5),(-100,50),'Time / ms','Membrane potential / mV',yticks=[-100,-70,-55,0,30,50]),
[ck('ResttopeakmV',30-(-70),100),yes('Undershootbelowrest',-80<-70)],'Voltage-gated sequence, trace interpretation and refractory behaviour are AHL. The pump maintains gradients but is not falsely assigned fast repolarization.','100 mV rise; Na+ influx depolarizes, K+ efflux repolarizes, and channel recovery limits immediate re-excitation.')

axon='<rect x="90" y="230" width="720" height="55" rx="24" fill="white" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'
for x in [110,340,570]:axon+=f'<rect x="{x}" y="200" width="195" height="115" rx="18" fill="#eee" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'
for x,label in [(95,'Node 1'),(322,'Node 2'),(552,'Node 3'),(790,'Node 4')]:
    axon+=text(x,160,label,18)
axon+=arr(140,375,350,375)+text(245,408,'local current',18)+text(450,465,'Insulating sheaths are interrupted by excitable nodes.',20)
add(744,'Myelin changes where action potentials are regenerated','C2.2','C2.2.9–11',
'A myelinated axon has successive excitable nodes 2.00 mm apart. The action-potential peak reaches node 1 at 0, node 2 at 0.040 ms and node 3 at 0.080 ms. When the segment between nodes 2 and 3 is demyelinated, arrival at node 3 is delayed to 0.240 ms; node 2 remains at 0.040 ms. Treat these times as conduction delays after equipment correction.',
[P('Calculate speed between nodes 2 and 3 before and after demyelination.',M('Distance = 0.00200 m; delays are 0.0000400 s and (0.240−0.040) ms = 0.000200 s.'),A('Normal speed = 50.0 m s−1; demyelinated speed = 10.0 m s−1.')),
 P('Explain saltatory conduction without claiming that a material particle jumps between nodes.',R('Action potentials generate local currents that depolarize the next excitable region.'),R('Myelin reduces current loss across internodal membrane, so threshold can be reached efficiently at the next node.'),R('The action potential is regenerated at nodes; ions themselves do not travel the full inter-node distance as a single jumping packet.')),
 P('Predict a possible severe effect of demyelination and identify two variables for a fair experiment.',R('Increased current leakage can leave the next node below threshold, causing conduction failure as well as slowing.'),R('Keep axon diameter and temperature constant, and compare the same path length with matched recording positions.'),R('Use independent axons and verify myelin damage rather than attributing all delay changes to myelin when nodal channels or cell viability may also be affected.'))],
svg(axon,'Nodes and local current in a myelinated axon'),
[ck('Normalspeed',.002/.00004,50),ck('Damagedspeed',.002/((.240-.040)/1000),10),ck('Arrivaldelayratio',(.240-.040)/.040,5)],'Saltatory conduction/local currents are AHL. Distance and milliseconds are converted correctly; depolarization propagates while individual ions do not jump the entire axon.','50.0→10.0 m s−1; local current regenerates spikes at nodes and severe leakage can cause failure.')

add(745,'Excitatory and inhibitory inputs combine before threshold','C2.2','C2.2.13–14',
'A constructed postsynaptic integration model starts at −70 mV with threshold −55 mV. A single excitatory input E causes a brief +6 mV change; a simultaneous inhibitory input I causes −8 mV. Within the specified short window, the model sums voltage changes linearly. Outside that window each effect has decayed. This is a supplied approximation for small postsynaptic potentials, not a claim that action-potential amplitudes add.',
[P('Predict whether three simultaneous E inputs reach threshold, and whether the same three E inputs plus I reach it.',M('Without I: −70+3×6 = −52 mV; with I: −70+18−8 = −60 mV.'),A('Three E inputs reach threshold; three E plus I do not.')),
 P('Distinguish spatial from temporal summation using this model.',R('Spatial summation combines inputs from different presynaptic neurons arriving at the same time.'),R('Temporal summation combines sufficiently closely spaced inputs from the same presynaptic source before earlier potentials decay.')),
 P('Explain inhibition and evaluate two limits of the linear calculation.',R('An inhibitory input moves potential farther from threshold or reduces the effect of excitation, decreasing the probability of firing.'),R('Real effects depend on synapse location, conductance and timing, so equal arithmetic changes are a model simplification.'),R('Once threshold triggers an action potential, its amplitude is not the sum of EPSP amplitudes; firing probability/frequency changes rather than creating a 3× action potential.'),R('Inputs separated by more than the decay window do not sum as assumed here.'))],
svg(arr(100,150,350,260)+arr(100,280,350,280)+arr(100,410,350,300)+box(350,205,240,150,['Integration region','rest −70 mV','threshold −55 mV'])+arr(590,280,790,280)+text(100,120,'E: +6 mV',19)+text(100,250,'E: +6 mV',19)+text(100,450,'E or I: see question',18)+text(735,325,'Axon output',19),'A model of converging postsynaptic inputs'),
[ck('ThreeEPSPpotential',-70+3*6,-52),ck('WithIPotential',-70+3*6-8,-60),yes('Thresholdclassification',-52>=-55 and -60<-55)],'Summation and inhibition are AHL. The linear small-potential approximation is supplied and distinguished from all-or-none action potentials.','Three E: −52 mV, fires; three E plus I: −60 mV, below threshold.')

add(746,'Blocking dopamine reuptake changes how long a synaptic signal persists','C2.2','C2.2.12–14',
'In a constructed dopamine synapse, equal transmitter release occurs at t=0. Extracellular dopamine concentration is 100, 37, 14 and 5 units at 0, 1, 2 and 3 s in control conditions, and 100, 74, 55 and 41 with a cocaine treatment that selectively blocks reuptake in this model. Receptor response remains above a supplied activation threshold when dopamine exceeds 20 units. Release is not repeated during the interval.',
[P('Use the sampled values to identify the last sampled time above threshold in each condition and calculate the treated/control concentration ratio at 3 s.',A('Control is above threshold at 1 s but not 2 s; treated is still above threshold at 3 s.'),M('Use 41/5.'),A('The 3 s concentration ratio is 8.20.')),
 P('Explain the mechanism without assuming more transmitter was released.',R('Reuptake normally removes dopamine from the synaptic region into the presynaptic side.'),R('Blocking this removal slows extracellular dopamine clearance, prolonging ligand availability to receptors after the same initial release.'),R('The initial concentration is the same, so the later difference need not be caused by a larger initial transmitter pulse.')),
 P('Evaluate the inference about signalling and its limits.',R('Above-threshold concentration for longer can prolong receptor stimulation in this supplied model.'),R('Concentration alone does not specify action-potential frequency or every behavioural effect; receptor state and network context also matter.'),R('Test transporter specificity, release amount and degradation independently; these are constructed mechanistic data, not a dosing or safety claim about cocaine.'))],
graph('Clearance after one equal dopamine pulse',[('Control',[(0,100),(1,37),(2,14),(3,5)]),('Reuptake blocked',[(0,100),(1,74),(2,55),(3,41)])],(0,3),(0,120),'Time after release / s','Extracellular dopamine / relative units'),
[ck('Treatedcontrol3sratio',41/5,8.2),yes('Controlthresholdcrosses1to2',37>20 and 14<20),yes('Treatedabovethreshold3s',41>20)],'Cocaine reuptake effects are AHL. The question does not conflate neurotransmitter concentration with action-potential amplitude, behaviour or medical advice.','Control falls below threshold by 2 s; treated remains above at 3 s; concentration ratio then is 8.20.')

add(747,'Stimulus intensity can be encoded by spike frequency, not spike height','C2.2','C2.2.10, C2.2.15–16',
'A pain-receptor model is recorded over equal 0.200 s windows. A weak stimulus produces 4 action potentials and a strong stimulus 14. Peak amplitude is approximately 100 mV in both. A selective local Na+-channel block eliminates spikes, while a nearby unblocked receptor still responds. The experiment measures peripheral receptor output, not a direct report of subjective experience.',
[P('Calculate the firing frequencies and their ratio.',M('Frequency = count/window duration: 4/0.200 and 14/0.200.'),A('Frequencies are 20.0 and 70.0 Hz; strong/weak ratio = 3.50.')),
 P('Explain the distinction between action-potential amplitude and frequency encoding.',R('An individual triggered action potential is approximately all-or-none, so a stronger stimulus need not make each spike taller.'),R('A stronger effective stimulus can cause threshold to be reached more often, increasing spike frequency.'),R('Refractory recovery limits how frequently the same axon can generate separate action potentials.')),
 P('Evaluate what the block and recording establish about pain and consciousness.',R('Loss of spikes after the selective block supports a role for local voltage-gated Na+ channels in transmitting the receptor output.'),R('A working neighbouring receptor helps control for general failure of the recording conditions but does not test every off-target effect.'),R('Peripheral spike frequency is input to central processing; subjective pain/conscious experience emerges from nervous-system activity and cannot be read off this one receptor trace alone.'))],
svg(ln(90,205,810,205)+ln(90,380,810,380)+text(100,145,'Weak',20,'start')+text(100,320,'Strong',20,'start')+''.join(ln(x,205,x,155) for x in [210,350,490,630])+''.join(ln(130+47*i,380,130+47*i,330) for i in range(14))+ln(120,445,780,445)+text(450,485,'Each displayed window spans 0.200 s; spikes have equal schematic height.',18),'Equal-duration pain-receptor spike-count windows'),
[ck('Weakfrequency',4/.2,20),ck('Strongfrequency',14/.2,70),ck('Frequencyratio',14/4,3.5)],'Pain-receptor coding, refractory limitations and emergence are AHL. The trace does not equate peripheral firing with a complete measure of conscious pain.','20.0 versus 70.0 Hz, ratio 3.50; intensity changes frequency rather than individual spike height.')

seedlings=''
for x,lab,bend,cap in [(160,'Intact',55,False),(450,'Opaque tip cap',0,True),(740,'Transparent cap',50,False)]:
    seedlings+='<path d="M'+str(x)+' 400Q'+str(x)+' 260 '+str(x-bend)+' 175" fill="none" stroke="#111" stroke-width="8"/>'
    seedlings+=text(x,460,lab,18)
    if cap:seedlings+=f'<path d="M{x-20} 185Q{x} 140 {x+20} 185Z" fill="#111"/>'
    else:seedlings+=circle(x-bend,172,12)
seedlings+=arr(75,95,825,95)+text(450,70,'Light travels from left to right',19)
add(748,'Tip shading and an auxin barrier test different stages of phototropism','C3.1','C3.1.17–21',
'Identical shoots receive light from the left. Mean curvature toward light after 4 h is 30° for intact tips, 2° for opaque tip caps and 28° for transparent caps. In separate shoots, an impermeable barrier under the tip on the shaded side gives 3°, while the same barrier on the lit side gives 25°. Each treatment has 12 independent shoots; reported values are means, not confidence limits. The schematic shows direction only, not the measured angles.',
[P('Compare the two cap treatments and calculate the reduction caused by the shaded-side barrier relative to intact shoots.',A('Opaque caps largely suppress curvature while transparent caps preserve a response close to intact shoots, supporting a need for light perception at the tip.'),M('Reduction = (30−3)/30×100.'),A('The shaded-side barrier reduces mean curvature by 90.0%.')),
 P('Explain a mechanism consistent with stronger effects of the shaded-side barrier.',R('Lateral light leads to unequal auxin distribution, with greater auxin effect on the shaded side of a shoot.'),R('Coordinated placement of auxin efflux carriers supports directional movement and maintains tissue concentration gradients.'),R('Greater shaded-side cell elongation bends the shoot toward the light; blocking movement on that side disrupts the asymmetry.')),
 P('Evaluate controls and a limitation of the evidence.',R('The transparent cap controls for mechanical covering while allowing light; the lit-side barrier helps distinguish barrier placement from a general barrier effect.'),R('Directly measure auxin distribution or carrier localization and elongation on both sides, because curvature alone does not prove the full molecular route.'),R('Means and sample size alone do not establish statistical significance; report variation and an appropriate analysis of independent shoots.'))],
svg(seedlings,'Shoot orientation and cap treatments; schematic curvatures'),
[ck('Shadedbarrierreductionpercent',(30-3)/30*100,90),ck('Capdifference',28-2,26)],'Phototropism/efflux/auxin action are AHL. Shoots bend toward left-originating light; the sketch is explicitly not an exact angle plot and no significance is inferred from means alone.','Opaque caps and shaded-side barriers suppress curvature; the barrier reduction is 90.0%, consistent with asymmetric auxin-driven growth.')

add(749,'An acidic wall can bypass one auxin-dependent growth step','C3.1','C3.1.20–22',
'A constructed shoot-segment experiment supplies turgor-supporting conditions and measures elongation over 2 h. Control segments elongate 1.0 mm, auxin-treated segments 4.0 mm, auxin plus a selective inhibitor of plasma-membrane H+ secretion 1.2 mm, and inhibitor plus an externally acidified cell wall 3.6 mm. The inhibitor does not remove cell viability or turgor. Root-tip-produced cytokinin and shoot-tip-produced auxin are also shown as transported signals in a separate schematic context.',
[P('Calculate the fraction of the auxin-induced extra elongation restored by external acidification relative to the control.',M('Auxin extra growth = 4.0−1.0=3.0 mm; acidified-wall extra = 3.6−1.0=2.6 mm.'),A('Restored fraction = 2.6/3.0 = 0.867, or 86.7%.')),
 P('Explain acid growth and the bypass result.',R('Auxin promotes H+ secretion into the apoplast, lowering cell-wall pH.'),R('Wall acidification promotes loosening of cross links between cellulose-containing wall components, allowing turgor-driven elongation.'),R('External acidification can bypass the inhibited H+-secretion step, supporting wall pH as part of the auxin growth mechanism under these conditions.')),
 P('Explain hormone coordination and evaluate a limit of the causal conclusion.',R('Shoot tips produce auxin transported toward roots, while root tips produce cytokinin transported toward shoots, coordinating growth across organs.'),R('The balance of these signals can influence root versus shoot growth; one local elongation assay does not by itself describe the entire plant response.'),R('The rescue is partial and conditional on maintained turgor/viability; independent replication and direct wall-pH measurement are needed, and acidification alone need not reproduce every auxin effect.'))],
bars('Shoot-segment elongation under bypass treatments',['Control','Auxin','Auxin +|H+ inhibitor','Acid wall +|inhibitor'],[1,4,1.2,3.6],'Elongation in 2 h / mm',5),
[ck('Baselinecorrectedgrowthrescue',(3.6-1)/(4-1),.866666666667),ck('Auxinextramm',4-1,3)],'Actual AHL auxin acid growth and auxin/cytokinin coordination are required. Rescue is baseline corrected; turgor is supplied, not incorrectly attributed to auxin loosening alone.','External acidification restores 86.7% of the auxin-induced extra growth; wall acidification supports turgor-driven expansion.')

add(750,'An ethylene pulse can trigger a self-reinforcing ripening response','C3.1','C3.1.19, C3.1.23',
'Matched unripe fruits receive a 1 h ethylene pulse, after which external ethylene is removed. Their own ethylene production at 0, 12, 24 and 36 h is 1, 3, 8 and 12 units. Controls without the pulse remain at 1, 1, 2 and 2. Fruits receiving the pulse plus a selective inhibitor of ethylene perception remain at 1, 1, 1 and 2. All chambers are ventilated identically after the pulse and production is normalized per kilogram fruit.',
[P('Calculate the 36 h production ratio for pulsed versus unpulsed fruit and describe the effect of receptor inhibition.',M('Use 12/2.'),A('The production ratio is 6.00; perception inhibition largely prevents the rising response despite the same initial pulse.')),
 P('Explain positive feedback in this ripening model and distinguish it from residual external gas alone.',R('Ethylene perception stimulates processes that increase ripening and further endogenous ethylene production.'),R('The new ethylene can reinforce the same response, amplifying the initiating signal through positive feedback.'),R('External gas is removed and ventilation is matched, so continued production measured later is not simply the original pulse remaining in one closed chamber.')),
 P('Evaluate one strength and two limits of the causal claim.',R('The perception-inhibitor comparison tests dependence on sensing the signal rather than only correlation between ethylene and ripening.'),R('Check inhibitor specificity and fruit viability; an unhealthy fruit could fail to produce ethylene for reasons other than selective feedback blockade.'),R('Use independent fruits matched for species, developmental stage and temperature, and measure ripening as well as gas production; the supplied gas time course alone does not establish every feature of ripening.'))],
graph('Endogenous ethylene after the external pulse is removed',[('Pulse',[(0,1),(12,3),(24,8),(36,12)]),('No pulse',[(0,1),(12,1),(24,2),(36,2)]),('Pulse + inhibitor',[(0,1),(12,1),(24,1),(36,2)])],(0,36),(0,16),'Time after pulse / h','Ethylene production / relative units kg−1',xticks=[0,12,24,36]),
[ck('Ethylene36hratio',12/2,6),yes('Pulsecontinuesincreasing',1<3<8<12)],'Ethylene positive feedback is actual AHL C3.1 content. Ventilation and normalization are explicit; perception dependence is not generalized without specificity controls.','Sixfold production at 36 h; endogenous ethylene can amplify ripening after the initiating pulse ends.')

def finish():
    assert len(Q)==50 and [q['number'] for q in Q]==list(range(701,751))
    assert Counter(q['level'] for q in Q)=={'sl':20,'ahl':30}
    assert len({q['stem'] for q in Q})==50 and len({q['title'] for q in Q})==50
    assert set(q['syllabusCode'] for q in Q)==set(SUB)
    for q in Q:
        assert q['marks']==sum(p['marks'] for p in q['parts'])==sum(len(g['steps']) for g in q['markScheme'])
        for p,g in zip(q['parts'],q['markScheme']):
            assert p['label']==g['part'] and p['marks']==len(g['steps'])
        fields=[q['title'],q['stem'],q['answerSummary'],*[p['prompt'] for p in q['parts']],*[s['text'] for g in q['markScheme'] for s in g['steps']]]
        for s in fields:
            assert not re.search(r'[\x00-\x08\x0b-\x1f]',s),(q['id'],'control character')
            assert not re.search(r'(?i)\b(?:significant|significance)\b.*(?:non.overlap|overlap).*prove',s)
    out=ROOT/'content/ib/biology-2026-expansion/interaction-interdependence.json'
    out.parent.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)
    out.write_text(json.dumps(Q,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n',encoding='utf8',newline='\n')
    report={'status':'author self-review passed; independent review pending','questions':50,'sha256':hashlib.sha256(out.read_bytes()).hexdigest(),'levels':dict(Counter(q['level'] for q in Q)),'difficulty':'hard','diagramCount':50,'substantiveNonTableDiagrams':50,'visualReview':'All 50 native SVGs rendered and inspected in five contact sheets; targeted synapse, capture-recapture, mitochondrial/thylakoid orientation, Calvin-cycle, intervention and multi-series plots also inspected at full size. Cleared labels from arrows, separated vesicle labels, replaced a pathway-order answer leak with intervention data, and made the CMR model a counted marked/unmarked figure.','marks':sum(q['marks'] for q in Q),'executableCheckCount':sum(len(r['checks']) for r in REV),'subtopics':dict(Counter(q['syllabusCode'] for q in Q)),'officialGuide':GUIDE,'correctedGuide':'https://xmltwo.ibo.org/publications/EAD/2026_EN/GR4/GR4-Biology_EN.pdf','specimen':SPECIMEN,'scope':'Current first-assessment 2025 Theme C; SL means shared content, AHL requires additional statements. Definitions and numerical stoichiometries beyond recall requirements are supplied explicitly.','provenance':'Original syllabus-authored IB-style practice with constructed data and native SVG; no official past-paper wording, licensed question-bank adaptation or invented published experiment.','limits':['M/A/R are instructional analytic points, not official IB markscheme notation.','Computational self-review and separate AI review are distinct; no human IB examiner moderation.','This author ledger does not claim integration, production build or deployment success.'],'records':REV}
    path=ROOT/'docs/ib-2026-biology/interaction-interdependence-author-review.json'
    path.parent.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)
    path.write_text(json.dumps(report,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n',encoding='utf8',newline='\n')
    print(json.dumps({k:report[k] for k in ['questions','sha256','levels','diagramCount','marks','executableCheckCount','subtopics']}))
if __name__=='__main__':finish()
